hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"16941","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that he used to equalize between teeth in blood money."
"16942","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled for the tooth five camels. Tawus said: And every tooth exceeds the next by what people of opinion and counsel deem."
"16943","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: I said to him: From where do we begin. He said: The two front teeth are the best of teeth."
"16944","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: For the tooth is five camels."
"16945","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, that Umar wrote to him: That teeth are equal."
"16946","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: For every tooth is five camels, and molars and teeth are equal."
"16947","From Malik, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Ghatfan: Marwan sent him to Ibn Abbas, asking him what he assigned for the molar. He said: Therein is five camels. He said: He sent me back to Ibn Abbas, he said: Do you make the front of the mouth like the molars. Ibn Abbas said: If you consider that only by the fingers, their blood money is equal."
"16948","From Ibn Jurayj and al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Muslim ibn Jundab, from Aslam, freedman of Umar, that Umar said: And for the molar is a camel."
"16949","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubrumah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab assigned for every molar five camels."
"16950","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The teeth. He said: For the two front teeth, the two side front, and the two canines, five five, and for the rest two camels two camels, upper mouth and lower, all that equal, and molars equal."
"16951","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled for fingers and teeth equal."
"16952","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: I heard Makhul saying: Fingers are equal, and teeth are equal. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me that he used to say like the saying of Ata."
"16953","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For the tooth is five camels, or its value in gold, silver or sheep."
"16954","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah saying: al-Harith ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Rabiah disputed me, with Alqamah, concerning teeth, he said: Muawiyah preferred molars over others. I said: No, and had he preferred, he would have preferred the front teeth."
"16955","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: In a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: And for teeth is five camels."
"16956","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Muslim informed me that he heard Tawus saying: The canine in the upper mouth and lower is preferred over molars. And that he said: For molars is small camels."
"16957","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The teeth of a woman all injured. He said: Fifty."
"16958","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Said informed me, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyib said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for what faces of the mouth, upper mouth and lower, five young she-camels, and for molars a camel a camel. Until when it was Muawiyah, and his molars were injured, he said: I know molars better than Umar, and ruled therein five five. Said said: Had the whole mouth been injured under the ruling of Umar, the blood money would decrease, and had it been injured under the ruling of Muawiyah, it would increase. Had I been, I would have assigned for molars two camels two camels, and that is the full blood money."
"16959","From al-Thawri, from Azhar ibn Muharib, he said: Two men disputed to Shurayh, one injured the front tooth of the other, and the other injured his molar, Shurayh said: The front tooth and its beauty, and the molar and its benefit, tooth for tooth, they are paired. al-Thawri said: And another said: The front tooth for the front tooth, and the molar for the molar."
"16960","From al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Makhul, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: For the tooth, respite is given a year, and if it blackens, therein is the full blood money, otherwise what blackens of it is by that reckoning. From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad, from Shurayh, the same."
"16961","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Respite is given a year, and if it blackens, therein is its blood money, otherwise therein is a judicial assessment."
"16962","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: And for the tooth is five camels, or its value in gold or silver. If it blackens, its blood money is complete. If broken of it when not blackened, then by that reckoning. And for the tooth of a woman is the same."
"16963","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: For the tooth, respite is given, and if it blackens between it and a year, its blood money is complete."
"16964","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If the tooth is split and does not blacken, then upon reckoning of what is lacking of it. And Qatadah said: What he breaks of the front tooth is by its reckoning."
"16965","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, he said: If a tooth falls, shakes or blackens, it is the same, it is dead."
"16966","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Ali, concerning a tooth that is struck. He said: If it turns black, then its full fixed compensation is due."
"16967","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me, he said: It suffices you that Abd al-Malik ruled concerning a tooth that is struck and turns black, with its full fixed compensation in full."
"16968","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me concerning a tooth: when it turns black, its full blood-money has become complete."
"16969","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, he said: among what was compiled for Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: if the tooth is struck and cracks while white and sound, with nothing falling from it, then for its crack is half its blood-money."
"16970","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, Abu Said said: I think it is from Ali, he said: concerning a tooth that is struck while they fear it will turn black, it is waited with for a year. If it turns black, then its full fixed compensation is due, and if it does not turn black, nothing is due. Abd al-Karim said: And they say: if it turns black after a year, nothing is due for it."
"16971","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled concerning the black tooth when it is broken, the sightless standing eye, and the paralyzed hand, with one-third of their blood-money. From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said, from Qatada, from Abd Allah ibn Burayda, from Yahya ibn Yamur, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, the same."
"16972","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: concerning the black tooth when it is broken, a just assessment is due."
"16973","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: concerning a tooth when it is struck, if it turns black, its full blood-money is due for it. Then if the second is struck, the full blood-money is due for it as well."
"16974","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: the black tooth, is it knocked out? He said: Something is due for it for its beauty and for blocking its place, and nothing has reached him on that. I said to him: Is something due for it even if its owner already took its fixed compensation? He said: Yes."
"16975","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, he said: if the tooth turns black or shakes, then is knocked out, half its fixed compensation is due, even if he had taken its fixed compensation the first time. As for Mamar, he mentioned from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: concerning the black tooth, a quarter of its blood-money."
"16976","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me that Abd al-Malik ruled concerning a tooth that is struck and turns black, with its full fixed compensation. Then if it is later knocked out and lost, its full fixed compensation is due for it."
"16977","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from one who informed him, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: concerning the black tooth that is knocked out, one-third of its blood-money."
"16978","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he ruled concerning the black tooth, when it is broken, with one-third of its blood-money."
"16979","Al-Hajjaj said: from Makhul, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: concerning the extra tooth, one-third of a tooth is due. From Ibn Jurayj, from Makhul, from Zayd, the same."
"16980","From Mamar, he was asked about a man who struck a man's tooth while it was loose. He said: Twenty-five dinars are due for it."
"16981","From Mamar, concerning a man who struck a man's tooth until it became loose. He said: An assessment is due for it. And he said: If it turns yellow, an assessment is due for it."
"16982","From al-Thawri, from al-Shabi, concerning the teeth of a child who has not yet shed his milk teeth, he said: Nothing is due on him. And another said: An assessment is due."
"16983","From Humayd, from al-Hajjaj, from Amr ibn Malik, that Umar ibn al-Khattab fixed for the teeth of a child who has not yet shed, one camel per tooth."
"16984","From Abu Hanifa, he said: An assessment is due for it. Zayd ibn Thabit said: Ten dinars are due for it."
"16985","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham informed me, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, that he fixed for the teeth of a small child who has not yet shed something he does not remember. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Makhul informed me that he said: for the teeth of one who has not yet shed, for every tooth a young she-camel, all equal."
"16986","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: if one strikes the teeth of a boy who has not yet shed, he said: he is waited with for a year. If they grow back, there is no blood-money for them and no retaliation."
"16987","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, concerning a boy who broke the tooth of a boy who has not yet shed, he said: compensation is due on him as the judge deems fit."
"16988","From Mamar, from a Kufan scholar, concerning the teeth of one who has not yet shed, for every tooth one camel is due. And another said: One-fifth of the blood-money for every tooth."
"16989","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said: concerning a tooth extracted in retaliation, then its owner puts it back in its place and it becomes firm, he said: there is no harm in that. Abd al-Razzaq said: Sufyan said: he extracts it another time."
"16990","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr informed me, from more than one, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that he said: it is not extracted. That was only where retaliation is not in pulling its root, like a hand that is broken and retaliation is taken from it, then the one retaliated upon heals, while the one for whom retaliation was taken becomes paralyzed. From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, like the saying of Ata. Mamar said: And it reached me from Ibn al-Musayyib that he said: it is extracted only once."
"16991","Sufyan said: concerning one who strikes a man's front tooth so it goes, he said: retaliation is taken from him, and he is not left to put his front tooth back in its place. He said: it is removed as his front tooth was removed. Then if one struck a man's front tooth and it grew back in its place, the one whose front tooth was struck may extract his other front tooth."
"16992","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: Safwan ibn Yala ibn Umayya informed me, from Yala ibn Umayya, he said: I campaigned with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the campaign of hardship. He said: Yala used to say: that campaign is my most reliable deed. He said: I had a hireling, and he fought a man. One of them bit the hand of the other, so the bitten one pulled his hand from the biter's mouth, pulling out one of his front teeth. They came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he voided his front tooth. He said: And I think he said the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Would he leave his hand in your mouth for you to chomp it, as if in a stallion chomping it?"
"16993","From al-Thawri, from Humayd al-Araj, from Mujahid, he said: A hireling of Yala ibn Umayya bit the hand of a man, so the other pulled his hand away, cutting off both his front teeth entirely. They came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: Does one of you bite his brother as a stallion bites, then seek blood-money? And he voided it."
"16994","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: a man bit a man and knocked out his front tooth, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, voided it and said: Did you want to chomp your brother's hand as a stallion chomps? From Mamar, from Qatada, from Imran, the same."
"16995","From Mamar, from Qatada, that Ali said: If you wish, offer your hand and let him bite it, then pull it away. And he voided his blood-money."
"16996","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulayka informed me that a man came to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and a man had bitten him. He pulled his hand away, and his tooth fell out. Abu Bakr said: His right hand is forfeit."
"16997","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abu al-Duha, he said: Shurayh said: Pull your hand from the lion's mouth."
"16998","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who bit a man so his finger became paralyzed, he said: his fellow retaliates. If it becomes paralyzed, retaliation is complete. If it is not paralyzed, his fellow pays him the blood-money of his paralyzed finger. Then if the hand of the one retaliated upon becomes paralyzed by what befell it, the blood-money applies for that, even if it reaches the life. For Allah is the one who commanded retaliation, and the retaliator owes only blood-money for an obligatory excess that befalls, not retaliation. But if the retaliator transgressed beyond his limit, his transgression is subject to retaliation."
"16999","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: the tongue cut off entirely? He said: The blood-money. I said: part of it cut off removing speech, with part of the tongue remaining? He said: I see only that the blood-money is due for it when speech is gone."
"17000","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the tongue the full blood-money. Then if its tip is cut and he makes some speech clear but not some, it is reckoned by letters. If he makes half the letters clear, half the blood-money; if he makes two-thirds clear, one-third of the blood-money."
"17001","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Mujahid, he said: when part of the tongue is injured, it is reckoned by letters, upon twenty-eight letters. He said: And another said: an assessment applies for that."
"17002","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed us that when what removes speech is cut from the tongue, the blood-money is due for it. I said: From whom? He said: It is the saying of the people. He said: Then if some speech goes and some remains, by the reckoning of speech, and speech is twenty-eight letters. I said: From whom? He said: I do not know."
"17003","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: in the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz to the military districts: what is cut from the tongue reaching prevention of all speech, the full blood-money is due for it, and what falls short of that, by its reckoning."
"17004","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled concerning the tongue, when it is cut, with the blood-money when it is torn from its root. And when its tip is cut yet its owner speaks, half the blood-money is due."
"17005","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: concerning the tongue when it is wholly uprooted, the full blood-money; and what is struck of the tongue reaching prevention of speech, the full blood-money is due for it; and for a woman's tongue, the full blood-money. And he narrated this whole account completely."
"17006","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, he said: for the tongue, the blood-money."
"17007","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, he said: Abu Bakr ruled: for the tongue when it is cut, the blood-money. Then if its tip is cut and he makes some speech clear but not some, half the blood-money."
"17008","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning the tongue of a non-Arab, one-third of the blood-money; and for the penis of a castrated man, one-third of the blood-money."
"17009","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Makhul, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled: concerning the tongue of a mute when wholly uprooted, one-third of the blood-money. Sufyan said: Concerning the mute's tongue and the castrated man's penis, a just assessment."
"17010","From more than one, from al-Hajjaj, from Makhul, from Zayd ibn Thabit: concerning the wry neck, when one cannot turn, the full blood-money."
"17011","From Mamar, he said: I heard that a man is struck and left wry-necked: half the blood-money is due for him."
"17012","Sufyan said: Concerning the wry neck, when one cannot turn, an assessment is due."
"17013","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: concerning the wry neck, when a man turns only tilted, half the blood-money: five hundred dinars."
"17014","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, I said: a blow removing the voice? He said: I heard nothing on that. Sufyan said: For the voice when it is cut off, an assessment is due."
"17015","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the voice when it is cut off by a blow, the full blood-money."
"17016","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz informed me, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that he said: concerning the throat when it is broken and the voice is cut off, the full blood-money."
"17017","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisa ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit: concerning a man who is struck until his intellect goes, the full blood-money; or struck until he becomes dazed and does not understand, the full blood-money."
"17018","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Abd al-Karim and Dawud ibn Abi Asim: concerning the voice when it is cut off, the full blood-money."
"17019","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Shabi, concerning the jaw when it is broken, forty dinars. From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from al-Shabi, the same."
"17020","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyib, concerning the two sides of a man's jaw, he said: he bends his thumb, then places its lower joint, opens his mouth, and places it between his jaw sides. Whatever his mouth-opening falls short of his thumb's lower joint, by reckoning."
"17021","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that he said: concerning the chin, one-third of the blood-money. Sufyan said: Concerning the chin, an assessment is due."
"17022","From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar and al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Muslim ibn Jundub, from Aslam the freedman of Umar, that he said: concerning the collarbone, one camel is due."
"17023","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: concerning the collarbone, I was told twenty dinars; and if there is malunion in it, forty dinars, for each of the two."
"17024","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed him, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: if the collarbone is cut and he does not live, the full blood-money is due for him. Then if he lives, fifty camels are due for it; and for both together, the blood-money."
"17025","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, from Amir and Mujahid, they said: if it is broken, forty dinars."
"17026","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, concerning its crack, four-fifths of its blood-money. Then if the arm is impaired due to the collarbone break, by the measure of what the arm is impaired of the arm's blood-money."
"17027","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, he said: concerning the collarbone, an assessment is due."
"17028","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: concerning a man's nipple? He said: I do not know."
"17029","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: concerning a man's nipple, five camels are due."
"17030","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, fixed for a man's nipple fifty dinars, and for a woman's nipple one hundred dinars. From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: and I heard him say the same. He said: And Ibrahim said: An assessment is due."
"17031","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled: concerning a man's breast, when its nipple is gone, with five camels."
"17032","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, concerning a man's breast, an assessment is due."
"17033","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, concerning a woman's two breasts, the blood-money; and for one of them, half."
"17034","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim, from Ibrahim, like the saying of al-Shabi: concerning a woman's two breasts, the blood-money; and for one of them, half the blood-money."
"17035","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisa ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd, he said: concerning the nipple of the breast, one-quarter of the blood-money."
"17036","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me that Abd al-Malik ruled in the fighting of Ghassan, when they struck the women, fixing for the breast fifty. I said to Dawud: the nipple of a man's or a woman's breast? He said: I do not know."
"17037","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled: concerning a woman's breast, with ten camels when only its nipple is struck. Then when it is cut from its root, fifteen camels."
"17038","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning the spine when it is broken, the full blood-money."
"17039","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning the spine when it is broken and his semen is gone, the full blood-money; and if the semen is not gone, half the blood-money. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled with that."
"17040","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, from Abu Bakr or from Umar, he said: if no child is born to him, the blood-money; and if a child is born to him, half the blood-money."
"17041","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: concerning the spine that is broken, the blood-money. I said to him: then it was broken and there is a bend in it? He said: No more than the blood-money is due, and if it heals straight, nothing is reduced from it."
"17042","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Sufyan informed me that Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Rabia said: I was present with Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr; he ruled concerning a man whose spine was broken and became hunchbacked, not bedridden, walking while hunched. He said: So he walked, and he ruled for him two-thirds of the blood-money."
"17043","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrima, that Abu Bakr or Umar ruled concerning the spine: when no child is born to him, with the blood-money; then if a child is born to him, half the blood-money."
"17044","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, he said: if the spine is broken and mends, and his semen is cut off, the full blood-money; and if his semen is not cut off, while there is a bend in the back, a wound to be assessed."
"17045","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Sufyan informed me that Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Rabia said: I was present with Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr; he ruled concerning a man whose spine was broken and became hunchbacked, not bedridden, walking hunched, with two-thirds of the blood-money."
"17046","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim said to me: if he cannot hold his legs steady, the full blood-money."
"17047","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled: concerning a man's spine when it is broken then mends, with the full blood-money if it does not support him, and with half the blood-money if it supports him."
"17048","From al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Makhul, from Zaida, that he said concerning the vertebrae: for every vertebra, thirty-one dinars and a quarter dinar."
"17049","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from al-Shabi that Zayd ruled concerning all the vertebrae of the back with the full blood-money, a thousand dinars; they are thirty-two vertebrae, each vertebra thirty-one dinars when it is broken then heals without malunion. Then if it heals with malunion, for its break thirty-one dinars and a quarter dinar, and for its malunion what its prospective assessment holds besides that. Abd al-Razzaq said: Sufyan said: Concerning a vertebra, an assessment is due."
"17050","From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar and al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Muslim ibn Jundub, from Aslam the freedman of Umar, he said: Umar said: concerning a rib, one camel is due."
"17051","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: concerning a rib when it is broken, one camel is due."
"17052","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a rib when it is broken then mends, twenty dinars. Then if there is malunion in it, forty."
"17053","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz informed me, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he ruled concerning a rib with one camel."
"17054","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, he said: concerning a rib, an assessment is due."
"17055","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: concerning a woman's rib when it is broken, ten dinars."
"17056","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: I heard Makhul say: when it is by mistake, one-third of the blood-money is due for it."
"17057","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: how much for the penetrating wound? He said: One-third. She said: Then it passed through from the other side. He said: Then perhaps two-thirds is due for it then."
"17058","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the penetrating wound, one-third; then if it passes through, two-thirds. From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, the same."
"17059","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Abu Bakr, he said: when it passes through, it is two penetrating wounds."
"17060","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: two penetrating wounds, then two-thirds of the blood-money is due for them."
"17061","From Mamar, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled for the penetrating wound with one-third of the blood-money."
"17062","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the penetrating wound in the side and the nose, one-third. Then if it passes through, two-thirds of the blood-money is due for it."
"17063","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, he said: with my father is a writing from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: for the penetrating wound, thirty-three."
"17064","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, he said: for the penetrating wound, one-third of the blood-money."
"17065","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Ibn al-Musayyib or another, that Abu Bakr ruled for the penetrating wound that passes through with two-thirds of the blood-money, when it pierces both sides and its man recovers. Sufyan said: I do not think, and a penetrating wound is only in the belly. We heard that."
"17066","From Ibn Jurayj and al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: for every piercing wound of a limb, one-third of that limb's blood-money is due."
"17067","From Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Said, he said: I heard the people say: for a penetrating wound discharging marrow, one-third."
"17068","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Said, he said: I saw the people fixing for the marrow-discharging penetrating wound one-third of that limb's blood-money."
"17069","From Ibn Abi Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that he said: when it passes through, two-thirds is due for it."
"17070","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled: concerning the penetrating wound in the belly that passes through, with two-thirds of the blood-money. And he said: They are two penetrating wounds."
"17071","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Dawud ibn Abi Asim, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: Abu Bakr ruled: concerning the penetrating wound, when it penetrates into the belly from both sides, with two-thirds of the blood-money."
"17072","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: concerning the penetrating wound in the belly, one-third of the blood-money: thirty-three camels, or their equivalent in gold, silver, or sheep. From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, the same. And: for a woman's penetrating wound, one-third of her blood-money."
"17073","From Mamar, from Sulayman ibn Habib, he said: Muawiya ruled: for every piercing wound in a marrow-discharging limb, one-third of that limb's blood-money. Then if it passes through from the other side, one-third and one-tenth of that limb's blood-money. And he ruled for every piercing wound in the belly with one-third and one-tenth of the blood-money."
"17074","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled: for the penis, with the blood-money."
"17075","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, that he ruled concerning the glans with the full blood-money."
"17076","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, he said: for the penis, the blood-money."
"17077","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, he said: with my father is a writing from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in it: when the penis is cut, one hundred she-camels are due, for his desire has ceased and his progeny is gone."
"17078","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: for the glans, the blood-money when it is struck. I said: Then the penis wholly uprooted? He said: The blood-money. I said: If the glans is uprooted, then something of what remains is later struck? He said: A wound to be assessed."
"17079","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, he said: for the penis, the blood-money; and for its glans alone, the blood-money."
"17080","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled: for a man's penis, with its blood-money: one hundred camels."
"17081","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, concerning the penis, the blood-money; and what is less than that, by its reckoning."
"17082","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: how much for the penis of a man who does not approach women? He said: Like that of one who approaches women. I said: The old man whose faculty has ceased, is his full fixed due not completed? He said: Yes."
"17083","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning the penis of one who does not approach women, one-third of that of one who approaches women. He analogized it to the sightless eye and the black tooth. He said: And likewise for the mute's tongue, one-third of the sound tongue's."
"17084","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man who heard Makhul say: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled: concerning the paralyzed hand, the mute's tongue, and the castrated man's penis when wholly uprooted, with one-third of the blood-money."
"17085","From al-Thawri: concerning the castrated man's penis, an assessment is due."
"17086","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: concerning the castrated man's penis, an assessment is due."
"17087","From al-Thawri and Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, he said: concerning a testicle, half."
"17088","From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: concerning the two testicles, the full blood-money."
"17089","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: the two testicles? He said: Fifty fifty, fifty for each testicle."
"17090","From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: concerning the two testicles, the full blood-money: fifty fifty. Ibn Jurayj said: I said to him: did you memorize whether distinction is made between the testicles? He said: No."
"17091","From Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Ashath ibn Sawwar, from al-Shabi, from Ibn Masud, he said: The two testicles are equal. From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, the same. From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the same."
"17092","From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: for the left of the two testicles, two-thirds."
"17093","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, from Umar, that he ruled concerning a testicle whose upper side is struck, with one-sixth of the blood-money."
"17094","From Mamar, from Ibn Rashid, from al-Shabi, he said: concerning the bladder when it is torn, one-third of the blood-money."
"17095","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from a man, from al-Shabi, that he said: concerning the bladder when it is torn and does not hold urine, one-third of the blood-money. He said: And I say: the full blood-money. And the Syrians said it."
"17096","From al-Thawri, he said: when a man cannot hold urine, the blood-money; and man and woman are equal in that. And he said: concerning one who cannot hold his stool, the blood-money."
"17097","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, he said: when one cannot hold his stool, the blood-money. From al-Thawri, the same."
"17098","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that he said: concerning the two buttocks when they are cut until the bone shows, the full blood-money; and for one of them, half."
"17099","From Mamar, from a man, Abd al-Razzaq said: I know him only as Abd al-Karim, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: concerning the two buttocks when they are cut until reaching the bone, the blood-money."
"17100","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim, from Ibrahim, he said: concerning the two buttocks, the blood-money."
"17101","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: how much for a woman's vulva? He said: I know nothing on it in our lands."
"17102","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Sufyan informed me, he said: it is ruled concerning the lip of her vulva, when wholly taken until reaching the bone, half her blood-money; and with her blood-money for her two lips, when reaching the bone, even if she is barren and does not bear."
"17103","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: it was compiled for Umar: concerning her pubic mound when it is cut, with the full blood-money, because it deprives the woman of the pleasure of intercourse."
"17104","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: concerning a woman's perforation, the full blood-money, because it prevents the pleasure of intercourse."
"17105","From Abd Allah ibn Muharrar, from Qatada, that Zayd ibn Thabit said concerning a woman perforated by her husband: if she retains the two excretions and the child, one-third of the blood-money is due for her; and if she does not retain the two excretions and the child, the full blood-money is due for her."
"17106","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled: concerning a woman who is overpowered and perforated, or her virginity removed, with one-third of her blood-money. And he said: No hadd is due on her."
"17107","From Mamar, from a man, from Qatada, concerning a man who injures a woman and perforates her, he said: One-third of the blood-money."
"17108","From Hushaym, from Dawud ibn Abi Asim, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb narrated to us that a man forced a woman and perforated her, so Umar ibn al-Khattab flogged him the hadd and fined him one-third of her blood-money."
"17109","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, the scholars compiled for him in his caliphate that concerning a prolapse caused by a blow, the full blood-money, because it prevents pleasure and intercourse."
"17110","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from al-Shabi, he said: concerning the shoulder when it is broken, forty dinars."
"17111","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, that it was agreed before Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: for the shoulder when broken then set without malunion, forty dinars. Sufyan said: For the shoulder there is judicial assessment."
"17112","From al-Thawri, from Zuhayr, from Abu Awn, from Shurayh, he said: For a rupture (fatq) there is one-third of the blood-money."
"17113","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: Whoever had a hand cut off in the path of Allah, then a person cuts off his other hand, he is fined two blood-moneys; but if his hand was cut off in a hadd punishment and then a person cuts off his other hand, he is fined the blood-money of the one he cut."
"17114","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a maimed man whose hand is then cut off, he said: If he were given the compensation of two hands, I would deem that not far from right, but I have not heard any Sunna on it."
"17115","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: For the hand uprooted there are fifty camels when cut from the shoulder, and the leg is the same."
"17116","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed for both hands the full blood-money, and for both legs the full blood-money."
"17117","From Mamar, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote them a document in it: the hand is fifty camels, and the leg is fifty camels."
"17118","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, he said: For the hand there is half the blood-money, and for the leg there is half the blood-money."
"17119","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata concerning the hand uprooted, fifty camels. I said: From where, from the shoulder or from the palm? He said: Rather from the shoulder."
"17120","Ibn Jurayj reported, Ibn Tawus informed me, he said: My father had a document from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it: for the hand fifty, and for the leg fifty."
"17121","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the hand there is half the compensation, and for the leg there is half the compensation, fifty camels, or its equivalent in gold, silver, cows, or sheep."
"17122","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: For the hand there is half the blood-money, and for the leg there is half the blood-money, or the equivalent of that in gold or silver."
"17123","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: For both hands the full blood-money, and for both legs the full blood-money. Mamar said: And I heard one say: If his leg loses a toe then a fifth of the leg blood-money, if it loses two toes then two-fifths of his leg blood-money, and if it loses three toes then three-fifths of his leg blood-money."
"17124","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: It is the same from wherever the hand is cut, from the shoulder or from below it down to the bracelet place, and the leg likewise from the thigh to the ankle."
"17125","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if the hand is cut from mid-forearm? He said: Fifty. I said: Then if something of what remains after is cut? He said: A wound, I deem it only that, unless a Sunna has passed on that."
"17126","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: Concerning the lame leg when he cannot step on it, its compensation is complete; and what falls short is by calculation of that."
"17127","Ibn Jurayj reported, Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid: If the palm is cut then fifty camels, and if what remains of the whole hand is cut except the forearm, or half the forearm is cut, then half of the hand due, twenty-five; and if it was cut from mid-forearm, or the forearm after the palm. Mujahid says that: Then half of the hand due, and if what remains after is cut then it is a wound to be assessed. I told it to Ata, and he said: I thought it was only a wound."
"17128","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, and from a man, from Ikrima, they said: For the hand when paralyzed, its full blood-money."
"17129","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shubruma, he said: When the leg falls short of its fellow, give him by account of what it fell short, or exceeded its fellow."
"17130","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar: For the hand and leg when deficient, by calculation."
"17131","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, he said: For the fingers, ten ten."
"17132","From Mamar, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote them a document in it: For the fingers of the hands and feet, for each finger thereof, ten camels."
"17133","He said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, Ibn Tawus informed me, he said: My father had a document from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it: For the fingers, ten ten."
"17134","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the fingers, ten ten for each finger, with no difference between them, or the value of that in gold, silver, or sheep. He said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled: For each finger, ten camels."
"17135","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: For each finger thereof, ten camels, or its equivalent in gold or silver; and for each phalanx cut from the finger phalanges, or paralyzed, a third of the finger compensation for each finger; cut from the fingers of the hand of a woman or her foot, or the equivalent of that in gold or silver; and for each phalanx of the finger phalanges of a woman, a third of the finger blood-money compensation, or the equivalent of that in gold or silver."
"17136","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar set for the thumb fifteen, for the index ten, for the middle ten, for the ring nine, and for the little six, until we found a document with the family of Hazm from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That the fingers are all equal. So he took it."
"17137","From Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from al-Ashath ibn Sawwar, from al-Shabi, that Ibn Masud said: The teeth are equal, the fingers are equal, the eyes are equal, the hands are equal, the legs are equal, and the testicles are equal."
"17138","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, that Umar wrote to him that the fingers are equal."
"17139","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: I heard Makhul say: For each finger ten, and for each tooth five camels; the fingers are equal and the teeth are equal. Muhammad said: And Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"17140","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Asim, from al-Shabi, he said: I bear witness over Masruq and Shurayh that they said: The fingers are equal, ten ten camels."
"17141","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: For each joint of the fingers, a third of the finger blood-money, except the thumb, for it has two joints, in each joint half."
"17142","From Mamar, from Qatada, and from a man, from Ikrima, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: For each fingertip, a third of the finger blood-money. He said: And in the hadith of Ikrima from Umar: Three she-camels and a third of a she-camel."
"17143","From Mamar, from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled on the fingers with a ruling, then he was told of a document the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for the family of Hazm: For each finger thereof, ten camels. So he took it and left his first order."
"17144","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: When the thumb and the one next to it are cut, in them is half the blood-money; and when one of them is cut, in it are ten camels."
"17145","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: In the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz to the troops: For each phalanx of the finger phalanges, when cut or paralyzed, a third of the finger blood-money, except what is of the thumb, for it is two phalanges, so in each phalanx of the thumb is half its blood-money."
"17146","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: For the paralyzed finger when cut, something for its beauty."
"17147","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: For the paralyzed hand, a third of its blood-money."
"17148","He said: Ibn Jurayj reported, from Dawud ibn Abi Asim, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for the paralyzed hand when cut, a third of its blood-money, and for the paralyzed leg, a third of its blood-money."
"17149","From Ibn Jurayj, from one who told him, from Ibn Shihab, that Umar ruled for the paralyzed hand when cut, a third of its blood-money, and for the paralyzed leg, a third of its blood-money."
"17150","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a paralyzed man whose sound hand is cut, he said: He is fined the blood-money of two hands."
"17151","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled: For the paralyzed hand when cut, a third of its blood-money. From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said, from Qatada, from Abd Allah ibn Burayda, from Yahya ibn Yamur, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar the same."
"17152","From al-Thawri, from one who told him, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar: For the paralyzed hand, the black tooth, and the standing eye, a third of its blood-money."
"17153","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning the eye whose light is gone, the black tooth, the paralyzed hand, and he mentioned the organ of the castrated, and the tongue of the mute: judicial assessment."
"17154","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning the paralyzed finger when cut, half its blood-money."
"17155","Ibn Jurayj reported, from a man, from Makhul, from Zayd, that he said: For the extra finger, a third of the finger blood-money."
"17156","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard from the people of knowledge saying: For the extra finger and the extra tooth when cut, or the tooth cast out, there is nothing in them, unless its place is disfigured, then it is assessed."
"17157","From Mamar, he said: It reached me: For the extra tooth and the extra finger, a third of its blood-money. He said: And Sufyan said: For the extra finger there is judicial assessment."
"17158","From a man, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning a man paralyzed in the fingers whose hand is cut deliberately, he said: He is compensated for the sound part in it, and for the paralysis there is settlement."
"17159","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: For the break of the hand and leg and collarbone, then set straight, there is something in that. And it has not reached me what it is."
"17160","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: When the hand or leg is broken, and when the forearm, thigh, upper arm, or shin is broken, then set and straightened, in each one there are twenty dinars. Mamar said: And it reached me that Qatada mentioned it from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Umar. Qatada said: But if there is malunion in it, then forty dinars."
"17161","Shurayh used to say: When it is set, there is nothing in it. He said: At that time its most severe."
"17162","Ibn Jurayj reported, Ikrima ibn Khalid informed me: That Nafi ibn Alqama was brought a case of a broken leg, and he said: We used to rule in it five hundred dirhams, until Asim ibn Sufyan told me that Sufyan ibn Abd Allah wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he wrote: Five uqiyas for the hand or leg broken then set and straightened. I said to Ikrima: So there is no crookedness nor paralysis? He said: Yes. He said: So Ibn Alqama ruled in it two hundred dirhams."
"17163","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ikrima ibn Khalid, from a man, from Umar, that he said: For the shin or forearm when broken then set and straightened without malunion, twenty dinars, or two hiqqas."
"17164","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Bishr ibn Asim informed me that a lad of theirs hired in Mecca, driving camels from his crops, and boys entered, so he chased them and struck one of them and crushed his upper arm, then it was set and straightened with no injustice in it nor harm, and Ibn Alqama ruled in it five hundred dirhams. So Amir wrote to him a letter, I do not know what it is, and Nafi returned it to two hundred dirhams."
"17165","From Ibn Uyayna, from Bishr ibn Asim, from Ikrima ibn Khalid, from Asim ibn Sufyan, that Umar wrote to Sufyan ibn Abd Allah concerning one of the two forearm bones, when healed without malunion, two hundred dirhams."
"17166","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mamar, from al-Jahshi, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, he said: Marwan ruled concerning a man who broke the leg of a man, then it was set, two faridas, meaning two she-camels (qalus)."
"17167","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: Sufyan ibn Abd Allah wrote to Umar, while his governor in Taif, consulting him concerning the hand of a man that was broken, and Umar wrote to him: If it was set sound, then he has two hiqqas."
"17168","Ibn Jurayj reported, and Dawud ibn Qays, from Sad ibn Said, brother of Yahya ibn Said, that Amra bint Abd al-Rahman told him, from Aisha, that she heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Breaking the bone of the dead, dead, is like breaking it alive, meaning in sin. Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad reported, from Sad ibn Said, from Amra, from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"17169","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard something concerning the nail, but I do not know what it is."
"17170","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: If the nail blackens or becomes defective, a she-camel."
"17171","Ibn Jurayj reported, Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, that he used to say: When the nail dries up, in it is a she-camel."
"17172","Ibn Jurayj reported, Abd al-Karim informed me, from Mujahid, that he used to say: If it does not regrow, two she-camels; and if it regrows crooked without luster, a she-camel."
"17173","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: If the nail regrows, a camel; and if it becomes defective, two camels."
"17174","Ibn Jurayj reported, Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Sufyan informed me, from Udhayna, that he used to say concerning the nail: When cast out and it does not regrow, a bint makhad; and if there is none, then an ibn labun."
"17175","Ibn Jurayj reported, Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Udhayna, that he used to say: In it is a small farsh of camels, meaning young."
"17176","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled concerning the nail when defective and spoiled, a qalus."
"17177","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled: For the nail when twisted and when spoiled, a qalus."
"17178","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz that it was agreed for him concerning the nail, when pulled out and it callouses, or falls, or blackens: a tenth of the finger blood-money, ten dinars."
"17179","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Amr ibn Harim, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, he said: For the nail when defective, a fifth of the finger blood-money."
"17180","Al-Hajjaj said, from Makhul, from Zayd ibn Thabit: For the nail uprooted: If it comes out black, or does not come out, in it are ten dinars; and if it comes out white, in it are five dinars."
"17181","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The blood-money of the man and the woman is the same until it reaches a third of the blood-money, and that is in the penetrating wound (jaifa); and when it reaches that, then the ransom of the woman is half the blood-money of the man."
"17182","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: A third of the blood-money of the man."
"17183","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, he said: Umar wrote to me five of the select rulings of the rulers: That the teeth are equal, the fingers are equal, and in the eye of a beast a fourth of its price; and concerning the man asked about his child at his death, the truest he is at his death; and concerning the wounds of men and women, the same, to a third of the blood-money of men."
"17184","Al-Thawri reported, from Rabia, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib: How much for a finger of the fingers of a woman? He said: Ten camels. He said: I said: For two fingers? He said: Twenty. He said: I said: For three? He said: Thirty. I said: For four? He said: Twenty. He said: I said: When her wound is great and her affliction severe, her compensation lessens? He said: Are you an Iraqi? He said: I said: Rather a knowledgeable verifier or an ignorant learner. He said: The Sunna. Mamar reported, from Rabia, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the same, except he said: I said: Now when her calamity is great and her wound severe, her compensation lessens? He said: From where are you? He said: I said: Either an ignorant learner or a knowledgeable verifier. He said: The Sunna, O nephew!"
"17185","Ibn Jurayj reported, Rabia informed me, that he heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: The man and the woman share compensation in what is below a third of his blood-money. He said: And I did not hear him ascribe it to anyone."
"17186","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa informed me, from Urwa, that he used to say: The blood-money of the woman is like the blood-money of the man until it reaches the third; and when it reaches the third, hers is like half the blood-money of the man; her blood-money in the penetrating wound and the skull-reaching wound is like half the blood-money of the man."
"17187","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: If two fingers of the fingers of a woman are injured together, in them are twenty camels; and if three are injured, in them are fifteen; and if four are injured together, in them are twenty camels; and if all her fingers are injured, in them is half her blood-money; and the compensation of the man and the woman is the same until it reaches the third, then the compensation of the man and the woman is separated at that, so it is separated, and the compensation of the man is in his blood-money, and the compensation of the woman is in her blood-money."
"17188","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I asked Ata: Till when does the woman share the man in compensation? He said: Her compensation is the same until it reaches a third of her blood-money and below; and when her wounds reach a third of her blood-money, in her wounds is half of his wounds."
"17189","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about four of her fingertips injured together. He said: In them is twenty."
"17190","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The compensation of the woman is like the compensation of the man until it reaches a third of her blood-money, and that is in the bone-displacing wound (manqula); and what exceeds the manqula is half the compensation of the man, whatever it is. From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"17191","From Mamar, from Qatada and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, they said: The woman shares the man in her wounds to a third of her blood-money. From al-Thawri, from Ibn Dhakwan, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz the same."
"17192","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Ali, he said: The wounds of the woman are half the wounds of the man. He said: And Ibn Masud said: They are equal in the tooth, the bone-revealing wound, and in what is besides that, half. And Zayd ibn Thabit used to say: To the third."
"17193","Mamar reported, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Masud, he said: They are the same to five camels. He said: And Ali said: Half in everything."
"17194","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The bone-revealing wound of the woman, her tooth, and her bone-displacing wound are equal to a third of the compensation."
"17195","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: To a third of the blood-money of the man."
"17196","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: I do not see the blood-money except for the paternal kin, for they pay for him; has anyone heard anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that? So al-Dahhak ibn Sufyan al-Kilabi, whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed over the Bedouins, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to me: That I give the wife of Ashyam al-Dibabi inheritance from the blood-money of her husband. So Umar took that. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar the same, and he added in it, and he said: By mistake."
"17197","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The woman, her paternal kin pay for her, and they inherit only what remains over her heirs, and they kill her killer; and the woman inherits from the property of her husband and his compensation, and he inherits from her property and her compensation, unless one of them kills the other. For the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The killer has no inheritance."
"17198","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Mughira ibn Shuba, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The woman, her paternal kin pay for her, and her sons inherit her."
"17199","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The compensation is on the paternal kin, and the blood-money is on the inheritance shares."
"17200","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, he said: The compensation is like the form of inheritance. I said to him: And do the uterine brothers inherit from it? He said: Yes."
"17201","Mamar reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama that he used not to give the uterine brothers inheritance from the blood-money."
"17202","Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that he heard Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib say: Ali said: The uterine brothers have been wronged by whoever does not give them inheritance from the blood-money."
"17203","From Mamar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to his governor concerning a woman whose husband was killed deliberately, or a man whose wife was killed deliberately: If they settle on the blood-money, then give him half of his wife blood-money, unless she has a child, then give him a fourth; and give her a fourth of her husband blood-money, and if he has a child then an eighth; and if they wish to kill, they kill, and if they wish to pardon, they pardon. He said: And a man of the people of al-Jazira told me that Umar wrote it to them."
"17204","Ibn Jurayj reported, Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he said: And he rules that all the heirs inherit from the compensation as they inherit from the inheritance. Ibn Tawus said: And I heard the people of Medina report that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave a woman inheritance from the blood-money of her husband, and a man from the blood-money of his wife."
"17205","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But if a woman is killed, her compensation is among her heirs, and they avenge her and kill her killer; and the woman inherits her husband from his property and his compensation, and he inherits her from her property and her compensation, unless one of them kills the other. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Compensation is inheritance among the heirs of the slain by their shares of inheritance, and what remains is for the paternal kin."
"17206","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And the paternal kin of the woman pay for her, whoever they are, and they inherit from her only what remains over her heirs."
"17207","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Concerning the man who kills his son deliberately: He inherits neither from his blood-money nor from his property anything; and if he kills him by mistake, he inherits from the property but does not inherit from the blood-money."
"17208","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, and from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, they said: Whoever kills a man by mistake inherits from his property but does not inherit from his blood-money; and if he kills him deliberately, he inherits neither from his property nor from his blood-money."
"17209","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that a man of Banu Mudlij killed his son, and Umar ibn al-Khattab did not retaliate against him, and fined him his blood-money, and did not make him inherit from him, and gave his mother and his paternal brother inheritance."
"17210","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, and from Qatada, they said: The name of the man killed is Arfaja. So Umar said: I do not retaliate for him. So Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Jushum said: O Commander of the Faithful, he killed him and he is dearer to him than his sight, but he had tribal zeal and killed him, not intending to kill him. So he ordered all his property, then thickened the compensation on him. They said: Who inherits him, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: In Arfaja there is dust. So he gave his mother and his brother inheritance."
"17211","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, and he mentioned that Qatada al-Mudliji had a slave girl who bore two men, and they grew up, then married. So his wife said: I am not pleased until you order her to herd sheep. So he ordered her. So his son said: We suffice what you burdened our mother. So their mother was not sent. So he ordered her a second time, and she did not do, and her son sent out to herd, so he angered, took the sword and struck the shin of his son, and it bled and he died. So Suraqa came to Umar ibn al-Khattab on that, and he said: Meet me at Qudayd with one hundred twenty camels, for I am descending on you. So he took forty pregnant she-camels, thaniyya to bazil of its year, and thirty jadhaa, and thirty hiqqa. Then he said to his brother: It is yours, and your father has nothing of it. And they mentioned they excused Qatada at Umar, saying: He did not intend him, he meant the hump and missed him. So Umar thickened his blood-money and made it quasi-deliberate."
"17212","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Rabbih ibn Said told me that Umar said, concerning the hadith of Qatada, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The killer gets nothing."
"17213","From Malik, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amr ibn Shuayb: That Suraqa ibn Jushum came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and told him: That a man of them called Qatada struck his son with a sword and hit his two shins, and he bled and died. So Umar turned away from him. So Suraqa said to him: If you are a governor you will face us, and if another, our affair is to him. He said: So Umar faced him, and presented the affair to him. So Umar said: Count for me at Qudayd one hundred twenty. When it came to him, he took from them thirty hiqqa, thirty jadhaa, and forty khalifa. Then he said: Where is the brother of the slain? Take! Then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The killer has no inheritance."
"17214","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that Umar said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The killer has no inheritance."
"17215","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, he said: A man killed his brother in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he did not make him inherit. He said: O Commander of the Faithful, I only killed him by mistake. He said: If you had killed him deliberately I would have retaliated against you. From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The killer has no inheritance. And he mentioned it from Ibn Abbas."
"17216","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The killer does not inherit from the slain anything."
"17217","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever kills a slain person does not inherit him, even if he has no heir besides him, even if his parent or child. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed that: The killer has no inheritance, and decreed that no Muslim is killed for a disbeliever."
"17218","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning one who kills his son deliberately, he said: He inherits neither from his blood-money nor from his property."
"17219","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The killer does not inherit from the slain anything, whether he kills him deliberately or kills him by mistake."
"17220","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: The killer does not inherit from the blood-money nor from the property, deliberate or by mistake. From al-Thawri, and we are on that, he never inherits in any case. From Hushaym, from Mughira, from Ibrahim the same."
"17221","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The killer, even by mistake, does not inherit from the blood-money nor from the property anything."
"17222","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, he said: The first ruled that the killer not inherit was in the fellow of Banu Israil."
"17223","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, he said in his hadith: So he was not made to inherit from him, and we do not know a killer inheriting after him."
"17224","From Uthman ibn Matar, or another, from Shuba, from Qatada, from al-Hasan: That a man threw his mother with a stone and killed her, and it was raised to Ali ibn Abi Talib, and he ruled on him the blood-money, and did not make him inherit from it anything. And he said: Your share of her inheritance is the ember. Or he said: The stone."
"17225","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: I will kill him. He said: That is not yours. I attended the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He retaliates the father for the son, and does not retaliate the son for the father."
"17226","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a slain person does not inherit him, even if he has no heir besides him, even his parent or child. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The killer gets nothing."
"17227","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa informed me, from Urwa, he said: We asked about the man who kills one he inherits by mistake, does he inherit from his blood-money anything? He said: No; and if that were allowed, a man would kill whom he dislikes of his kin."
"17228","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who killed his father or brother, he said: The predecessors of this community used to thicken the blood-money on them; the imams suspected them."
"17229","From Sufyan, that he said: Concerning a man who killed his son deliberately, he said: The blood-money is in his property especially, nothing on the paternal kin; and if by mistake, it is on the paternal kin."
"17230","From Muhammad ibn Yahya, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmala, that he heard a man of Judham narrate, from a man of them called Adi, that he threw his wife with a stone and she died. So he followed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Tabuk and told him his affair. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: You pay for her, but do not inherit her."
"17231","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abbas ibn Abd Allah informed me that Umar said: Concerning one who kills deliberately, then retaliation does not fall on him, he is flogged a hundred. I said: How? He said: In the free who kills the slave deliberately, and the like of that."
"17232","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ismail ibn Umayya, he said: I heard that one who kills a slave is imprisoned and beaten a hundred."
"17233","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab beat a freeman who killed a slave a hundred, and exiled him a year."
"17234","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: If a freeman kills a slave deliberately, he is punished with painful flogging, prison, and freeing a neck; and if he does not find, then fasting two consecutive months; and if he kills him by mistake, he is ordered to free a neck or fast two consecutive months, and there is no punishment on him."
"17235","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no retaliation between the freeman and the owned, but punishment, deterrence, payment of what he damaged, and freeing a neck. And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled so."
"17236","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The paternal kin does not bear confession."
"17237","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled that the paternal kin does not bear confession nor settlement unless they wish."
"17238","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The blood-money is on the guardians in every offense he commits."
"17239","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, he said: Four, there is no compensation in them on the paternal kin; it is in his own property: the deliberate, confession, settlement, and the owned slave."
"17240","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The deliberate, quasi-deliberate, confession, and settlement the paternal kin does not bear for him; it is on him in his property unless the paternal kin helps him; and they must help him, as it reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his document he wrote between Quraysh and the Ansar: Do not leave a burdened one without helping him in ransom or compensation. He said: And the burdened is all that the paternal kin does not bear."
"17241","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: They may not forsake him in anything befalls him, meaning in settlement."
"17242","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: Quasi-deliberate is on the man in his property, not the paternal kin. Sufyan said: And our companions deem that on the paternal kin."
"17243","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The paternal kin does not pay what is below the bone-revealing wound, nor deliberate, settlement, nor confession."
"17244","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Shabi, he said: Every wound from which no retaliation is taken is from the property of the culprit when deliberate. And Ibn Jurayj said it, from Ata."
"17245","Sufyan said: What is below the bone-revealing wound is on the culprit, and the bone-revealing wound and above is on the paternal kin. And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled: With the bone-revealing wound on the paternal kin."
"17246","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: A third and below is in his own property, and what exceeds is on the paternal kin."
"17247","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When it reaches the third, it is on the paternal kin. He said: And Ibn Ayman said that to me, and I do not doubt he said: And what does not reach the third is on the folk of the man especially."
"17248","From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, he said: They are agreed, or Abd al-Razzaq said: We almost agreed that what is below the third is in his property especially. Sufyan said: In the offense of the boy, what is property is in his property, and what is wounds is on the paternal kin. He said: And Ibn Abi Layla said: In a boy who deflowers a girl, it is in the property of the boy."
"17249","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a man kills a slave by mistake, it is on his paternal kin; and if he kills a beast by mistake, it is on his paternal kin. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar and Sulayman ibn Musa said: The paternal kin does not bear it; it is on him in his property, for it is property."
"17250","From Mamar, from some scholars of Kufa, he said: The bone-revealing wound and above is on the paternal kin, when by mistake."
"17251","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, concerning the man who kills the man deliberately and he accepts the blood-money from him, he said: The paternal kin does not pay it unless they wish. He said: And confession is likewise. He said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled so."
"17252","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard him, or he said: It reached me from him, he said: A third and below is in his own property."
"17253","From Zama, from Ziyad al-Khurasani, from al-Zuhri, he said: A third and below is from his own property, and what exceeds that is on the people of the register."
"17254","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, concerning the man who injures himself, they said: From Umar: A hand from the hands of the Muslims."
"17255","Mamar reported, from Qatada: That a man gouged his own eye by mistake, and Umar ruled for him its blood-money on his paternal kin."
"17256","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There was a rajiz who used to recite rajaz verses for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Then his son came after he died and said: May I recite rajaz for you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. He said: Then Umar said: Watch what you say. He said: I say: By Allah, had it not been for Allah we would not have been guided. Umar said: You spoke the truth. And we would not have given charity nor prayed. Umar said: You spoke the truth. So send down tranquility upon us and make firm our feet if we meet (the enemy), and the polytheists have transgressed against us; when they say, disbelieve, we refuse. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who says this? He said: My father, O Messenger of Allah, said it. He said: May Allah have mercy on him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, people refuse to pray over him, fearing he killed himself. He said: No, rather he died as a mujahid; he has two rewards. Al-Zuhri said: He had struck a polytheist with his sword and his sword struck himself, so he died."
"17257","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who killed a man deliberately, then fled and could not be caught until he died, leaving wealth, he said: They have nothing but retaliation (qawad, which is now missed)."
"17258","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a man kills a man deliberately then flees and cannot be caught until he dies, leaving wealth, then the blood-money of the slain is in his wealth. It was said to him: Was the killer imprisoned until he died? He said: They killed him — they imprisoned him in prison until he died; and I say: If they imprisoned him to verify his case, but did not verify, then evidence was established after he died that he killed, the blood-money of the slain is in his wealth; but if they imprisoned him after verifying that he was the killer, until he died, then the slain has no right."
"17259","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, he said: I asked him about a man, if he kills someone — is the blood-money paid from his own wealth, or does his clan (ashirah) pay it for him? He said: Whatever is deliberate, the clan does not pay it, unless they wish."
"17260","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Everything in which there is no retaliation, its blood-money is in the wealth of the offender; if he has no wealth, then it is upon the aqilah (paternal relatives who bear blood-money) of the offender. If he cuts his right hand deliberately, while the cutter's right hand had already been cut before that, then its blood-money is in the wealth of the cutter; if he has no wealth, then upon his aqilah. If he has a left hand, it is not cut in retaliation for it. And the blood-money is likewise for all the limbs. Ibn Shihab said the same."
"17261","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who kills his son by mistake, he said: His aqilah pays his blood-money if evidence is established. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, the same as the saying of al-Zuhri."
"17262","Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Ata: And a slave who kills his son, a free person? He said: He must be compensated for (he must be paid for)."
"17263","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who puts out his son's eye by mistake, or breaks his hand by mistake, he said: If evidence is established for that, its compensation is upon his aqilah; and if no evidence is established, he has nothing, except from his own private wealth."
"17264","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata say: The son is not subjected to retaliation (for the father) from his father, but the wife is subjected to retaliation (for her husband) from her husband."
"17265","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man injures himself with a wound by mistake? He said: His aqilah pays it. It is said: A hand from among the hands of the Muslims. Then he informed me: Once a man was riding his beast and struck it, and the tip of his whip came back and put out his eye. Amr ibn al-As wrote about it to Umar, and Umar wrote: If evidence is established that he injured himself by mistake, then let him be compensated. Umar said: A hand from among the hands of the Muslims. He said: As for Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: A man struck his beast with a stick and it came back upon his eye. Then he narrated similar to this."
"17266","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, concerning a man who kills a man by mistake, then kills another deliberately, he said: He is killed, then the blood-money for the mistake is upon his aqilah; and if he kills a man deliberately, then kills another by mistake, it is likewise. Qatadah said: And Ata ibn Abi Rabah said: I would not deprive the family of the first of the blood-money, when retaliation is missed for them. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said it too."
"17267","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who kills a man deliberately, then is imprisoned, and a man comes and kills him by mistake, he said: The blood-money is upon the one who killed him, for the heirs of the man who killed deliberately, since the retaliation that was due upon him was missed for them."
"17268","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: If a man kills a man by mistake, then kills another deliberately, then the mistake is to be compensated, because its compensation had been established before the deliberate act."
"17269","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Concerning a man who kills a man deliberately, then another comes and kills the killer deliberately, he said: The family of the slain who was killed has the blood-money upon their killer."
"17270","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If he kills deliberately, then he himself is killed by mistake, then they both have the blood-money, when the first retaliation is missed for them. Qatadah said likewise, from Ata."
"17271","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: They only had retaliation, so they have nothing; rather his blood-money is for the heirs of the one who killed by mistake. And this is the saying of Qatadah."
"17272","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who kills a man deliberately, then kills another by mistake, he said: He is killed for him, and the blood-money is for the first ones upon those who exacted retaliation from their man. Ma'mar said: And al-Hasan said: No retaliation and no blood-money."
"17273","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, concerning a man who kills a man deliberately, then another comes and kills him by mistake, he said: The blood-money is for the family of the first."
"17274","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who kills deliberately, then kills by mistake, he said: He is not compensated for, because he has forfeited (locked up) his blood-money."
"17275","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who kills a man, then a man comes and kills the killer, he said: The one who killed him is killed for him, and the blood of the first is void; they only had retaliation, and it was missed for them."
"17276","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who kills a man, and the heir of the slain meets him and kills him without informing the authority — or he said the imam — he said: Upon him is punishment, but he is not killed."
"17277","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, concerning a man who steals, and a man assaults him and cuts off his hand, he said: The hand of the one who assaulted him is cut off, and the foot of the thief is cut off. Ma'mar said: And I heard someone say: Upon the one who cut the thief is the blood-money, and upon the thief is nothing besides what was done to him. He said: And Ibn Abi Layla said, concerning a man who cut off a man's hand, then the father of the one whose hand was cut came and cut off the hand of the cutter, he said: He is cut off."
"17278","From al-Thawri, he said: When the thief is cut (his hand cut by a vigilante) and the adulterer is killed before informing the authority, upon him is retaliation; and upon the thief and the adulterer is nothing besides that, because what was due upon them has already been taken from them. But if the apostate is killed before he is raised to the authority, then upon his killer is nothing."
"17279","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who kills a man leaving two brothers, and one of them pardons, then the other kills him before raising him to the imam, he said: It is a mistake; upon him is the blood-money — half the blood-money is taken from him."
"17280","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The people refuse to pay blood-money for their freedman — does he become the freedman of whoever pays it for him? He said: Muawiyah said: Either they pay blood-money for him, or we pay blood-money for him, and he is our freedman. Ata said: If his family refuses to pay for him, and the people refuse to pay for him, then he is the freedman of the victim."
"17281","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled that whatever harm any Muslim suffers of blood-money due upon him for anything — if he suffers it, it is a compensation upon his aqilah, if they wish; and if they refuse, they may not abandon him in anything that befalls him."
"17282","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: The blood-money is upon his heirs for every offense he commits."
"17283","From al-Thawri, he said: If the aqilah refuses to pay blood-money for their freedman, they are compelled to do so."
"17284","From Ma'mar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote that freedmen — their lineages do not bear their blood-money liabilities; rather it is upon their patrons and their aqilah."
"17285","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Abu Wail that Umar ibn al-Khattab set the full blood-money in three years, and set half the blood-money in two years, and less than half in one year. Ibn Jurayj said: And Umar set two-thirds in two years."
"17286","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Shabi, that Umar set the blood-money in the stipends (ata) in three years, and the half and the two-thirds in two years, and the third in one year, and less than a third in its (current) year."
"17287","From al-Thawri, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Muhammad ibn Rashid, that he heard Makhul narrate it, from Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The blood-money is twelve thousand upon the people of dirhams, and upon the people of dinars one thousand dinars, and upon the people of camels one hundred camels, and upon the people of cattle two hundred cows, and upon the people of sheep two thousand sheep, and upon the people of garments two hundred hullahs (suits). And he ruled the blood-money: two-thirds in two years, half in two years, a third in one year, and whatever is less than a third is in that same year."
"17288","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, or another, from al-Nakhai, he said: If it is a third of the blood-money, then in one year; and if it is two-thirds of the blood-money or half the blood-money, then in two years."
"17289","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: The blood-money is taken in three years."
"17290","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jafar say: Uthman ruled that any man who sits with a blind man and is harmed by him with something, it is void (hadar, no liability)."
"17291","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The one leading (the beast) pays for (injury by) its foreleg, but does not pay for (injury by) its hind leg. He said: I said to him: If the beast was unruly and struck a person with its foreleg while being led? He said: The one leading pays."
"17292","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does the driver pay for the foreleg and the hind leg? He said: They claimed he pays for the foreleg. I debated him, and he said, saying: The road, the road (i.e., warn people to clear the way)."
"17293","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: The one leading pays for whatever (the beast) tramples with foreleg or hind leg; but if it kicks, he does not pay. He said: And the rider is likewise, except if he pulls the rein so it kicks, then he pays."
"17294","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning two riders on one beast, he said: If their beast harms someone, they both pay together."
"17295","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, he said: The one riding behind guarantees along with his companion. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, the same."
"17296","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you consider the rider like the one leading regarding payment for (injury by) its foreleg? He said: Yes."
"17297","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Shurayh, he said: The one leading, the driver, and the rider guarantee; but the beast is not guaranteed for if it retaliates. I said: What is retaliates? He said: When a man strikes it and it hits him."
"17298","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Rahman, from Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: A man pricked a beast upon which was a rider, and it kicked a person and wounded him. They came to Salman ibn Rabiah, and he said: The rider pays. Then they came to Ibn Masud, and he said: The pricker pays."
"17299","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, he said: A girl rode upon (the back of) a girl, and another pricked her (the mount), so she fell and died. He held (liability) upon the pricker and the one who was pricked (the carrier)."
"17300","From al-Thawri, from Abu Qays, from Hudhayl ibn Shurahbil, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The mine is jubar (no liability), the well is jubar, the stray beast is jubar, and in buried treasure is the fifth; and the foot is jubar — meaning the foot of the beast; and jubar is hadar (void, no compensation)."
"17301","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Farwah, he said: I heard al-Shabi say: The foot is jubar."
"17302","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: If it kicks a person, there is no liability upon him. Sufyan said: And its interpretation with us is when it was walking; and other than al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim: And he held him liable for what it struck with its foreleg."
"17303","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a leader and a rider who trample a person, he said: The leader and the rider pay; but if the rider was blind and cannot see, or sick and cannot turn his beast away from the man whom the leader pressed upon him, then we consider the payment upon the leader."
"17304","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a person riding with a spear, and the spear hits a person, he said: He guarantees."
"17305","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who was leading beasts, and (other) beasts followed them and harmed a person, he said: He guarantees; but if it broke loose, there is no liability upon him."
"17306","From Yahya, from al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: If a man goes out on a beast followed by its foal, and the foal harms a person, he said: He guarantees. Sufyan said: If the beast breaks loose, there is no liability upon its owner."
"17307","From Ma'mar, he said: I do not know al-Zuhri except that he said: If he was driving or riding, and the beast harms with its foreleg or its hind leg, he pays; but if he was leading, there is no payment."
"17308","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man orders his slave to kill a man? He said: (Liability) is upon the one who orders. I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The free commander is killed, and the slave is not killed. What do you think — if a man sends a gift with his slave to a man, who gave it? I said: Then he orders his hired man? He said: I consider his hired man like his slave. I said: Then he orders a free man, or a slave he does not own, and they are not hired men? He said: (It is) upon the one ordered, if he does not own them or they are hired men. Ata said afterwards: If he orders a free man, the free one ordered is killed; and nothing reached him concerning a slave other than his, nor concerning the hired man."
"17309","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who orders a free man, and he kills a man, he said: The killer is killed, and upon the one who orders is nothing."
"17310","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: If a man orders a slave of his and he kills a man, the one who orders is not killed, but he pays his blood-money, and he is punished and imprisoned. But if he orders a free man, then the free man — if he wishes he obeys him, and if he wishes not — so the one who orders is not killed."
"17311","From Ma'mar, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, concerning a man who orders his slave and he kills a man, he said: The slave is killed and the master is punished."
"17312","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, concerning a man who orders his slave and he kills a man, he said: The slave is killed, and upon the master is nothing. Sufyan said: And we consider that upon the master is his chastisement (tazir)."
"17313","Sufyan said, concerning one who says to another man's slave: Kill your master, and he kills, he said: Upon him is no compensation, and it does not remove him from anything (of ownership); but the one who orders is chastised. And if he says to another's slave: Kill so-and-so, and he kills him: the slave is killed, and the one who orders is fined the price of the slave to the slave's master."
"17314","Sufyan said, concerning one who says to another man's slave: Kill so-and-so by mistake, and he kills him, he said: Upon the one who orders is nothing."
"17315","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who orders his slave to kill a man, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The free one who orders is killed, and the slave is not killed. Did Abu Hurayrah say: What do you think — if a man sends a gift with his slave to a man, who gave it?"
"17316","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: He orders a man he does not own, who has reached (the age) to run a horse for him, and he dies during that running of his? He said: They claimed it is upon the one who orders."
"17317","From Abu Hanifah, and he was asked about a man who takes a boy without his family's permission and runs a horse for him, and he dies, he said: He guarantees."
"17318","Al-Thawri said: Concerning a man who orders a boy to kill a man, he said: His compensation is in the wealth of the boy, and the one who ordered him pays him the like of his compensation."
"17319","From Ma'mar, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The killer is killed, and the one who confines (holds the victim for the killer) is confined."
"17320","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man holds a man so another kills him? He said: Ali said: The killer is killed, and the holder is imprisoned in prison until he dies. I said: If they attain from him something less than his life? He said: Retaliation is taken from the assailant, and the holder is punished. I said: Then if he kills him outright? He said: Then the holder is not killed either. He said: He did not hold him, nor guide, but he walked with the killer, and spoke, and prevented him from a blow intended by him? He said: He is not killed — meaning the assailant who assaults with his hand and strikes until he kills."
"17321","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Ali ruled that the killer is killed, and the holder is imprisoned unto death."
"17322","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah informed me, a report he affirmed from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The confiner is imprisoned unto death as he confined, and the killer is killed."
"17323","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: If he seeks the help of a free man who has attained understanding, for help, and he dies, he does not pay for him. And Amr — I said to Ata: What is its time? He said: That he understands."
"17324","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, he said: Whoever seeks the help of a slave or a boy without his family's permission has guaranteed him. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, the same."
"17325","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, concerning a man who orders two boys to wrestle, and one of them wounds his companion, he said: The blood-money of the wounded is upon the wounder, and the man who ordered him pays him the like of that."
"17326","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: Whoever seeks the help of an owned slave without his masters' permission guarantees."
"17327","Abu Hanifah said, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: Whoever seeks the help of an owned slave without his family's permission guarantees. He said: And the boy is in that position."
"17328","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and he was asked about a man who seeks the help of people to demolish a wall, and some of them perish in it, he said: Upon the one who sought their help is nothing; and it is upon their companions who survived the wall (and) did not help."
"17329","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I hire a boy for work his family knows he does, and that work kills him? He said: He pays. I said to him: They let him earn and work, and I hired him and his work kills him? He said: He does not pay. I said: A servant of people who did not permit him work, and I hired him for work without their order? He said: He pays."
"17330","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, concerning a man who hires workers to dig a well, or demolish a wall, and the wall falls upon them and some of them die, they said: Upon the one who hired them is no liability; but the living among them pay the blood-money of the dead."
"17331","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One of those taken from said to me: If two men dig at the base of a wall and it falls upon them, and one of them dies, the blood-money is halves between them."
"17332","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who hires workers to work for him, and they lift a stone, and are unable for it, and the stone falls upon some of them, he said: Upon the one who hired them is no payment; rather the payment is upon whoever forced (overburdened); and if some of them forced some, upon him is what befell."
"17333","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who hires people to demolish a wall for him, and a side of the wall falls and kills some and wounds some, he said: They are given the blood-money of their slain, and they pay the wounds of those wounded among them."
"17334","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man calls to a boy upon a wall to step back, and he falls and dies? He said: It is narrated from Ali that he said: He pays for him. He said: He frightens him. I said: Then he calls to an adult? He said: I do not consider him but like him. I debated him, and he considered that he pays."
"17335","From al-Thawri, from Ismail, from al-Shabi, asked about a slave who kills a man, then his master frees the slave, he said: Upon the master is the blood-money. He said: And al-Hakam and others said: The price — the price of the slave. He said: And they say: If he knew, then the blood-money; and if he did not know, then the value."
"17336","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umarid informed me that Huyayy ibn Yala informed him that he heard Yala inform that a man came to Yala and said: The killer of my brother. He handed him over to him, and he struck him with the sword until he thought he had killed him, while a breath of life remained in him. His family took him and treated him until he recovered. Then Yala came and said: The killer of my brother. He said: Did I not hand him over to you? He informed him of his story. Yala called him, and he had healed, his wounds stuffed. He found in him the blood-money. Yala said to him: If you wish, hand over to him his blood-money and kill him; and if not, leave him. He caught up with Umar and demanded fulfillment against Yala. Umar wrote to Yala: Come to me. He came to him and informed him of the story. Umar consulted Ali ibn Abi Talib, and he advised him with what Yala had ruled. Umar and Ali agreed upon the ruling of Yala: that he hand over to him the blood-money and kill him, or leave him and not kill him. Umar said to Yala: Indeed you are a judge. Then he returned him to his post. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Ikrimah ibn Yala: That this killer was treated by his family and recovered; they brought him to Yala, and he mentioned; and Umar refused to kill for them the second time."
"17337","Sufyan said, concerning one who is not finished off and recovers, he said: He is killed and pays no wound (compensation)."
"17338","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man finds a man with his wife and kills him — is he void (no liability)? He said: Nothing of command except with evidence."
"17339","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, that he used to deny that Umar voided his blood except with evidence."
"17340","From Ibn Jurayj and al-Thawri, they said: Yahya ibn Said informed us, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: A man from the people of Sham called Jubayr found a man with his wife and killed him — or killed them both, al-Thawri said: So he killed him — and Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, found the ruling difficult, so he wrote to Abu Musa al-Ashari to ask Ali about that for him. He asked Ali, and he said: This is not in our land; you shall surely inform me. He said: He wrote to me to ask you about him. He said: I am Abu Hasan al-Qarm — he is handed over in his entirety (executed) unless he brings four witnesses. From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the same."
"17341","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the man said: He finds a man with his wife and kills him? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Except with evidence. Sa'd ibn Ubadah said: And what evidence is clearer than the sword? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you not hear what your chief says? They said: Do not blame him, O Messenger of Allah, for he is a jealous man; by Allah, he never married a woman except a virgin, nor divorced a woman ever, so any one of us was able to marry her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah refuses except with evidence."
"17342","From Ma'mar, from Kathir ibn Ziyad, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who finds a man with his wife, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sword suffices as wi — intending to say witness — but he did not complete the speech until he said: Then the drunk and the jealous would deal in it (i.e., misuse it)."
"17343","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: I think from Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: A man sought hospitality from people of Hudhayl, and they sent a slave-girl of theirs to gather firewood. She pleased the guest, so he followed her and wanted her for himself. She refused, and he wrestled her a while. She slipped from him, threw a stone at him, split his liver, and he died. Then she came to her family and informed them. Her family went to Umar and informed him. Umar sent and found their traces. Umar said: The slain of Allah is never compensated. Al-Zuhri said: Then the judges after ruled that he is compensated."
"17344","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abu Abdullah ibn Ubayd narrate similar to this, and I say, and the companion of Iraq: And Ashath — Islam deceived him from me — I dallied with his bride on the night of full (moon); I spend the night upon her breasts, while he folds upon Hamra who meets him the belt; as if the gatherers of lust from her are troops rising against troops."
"17345","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah ibn al-Numan, from Hani ibn Hizam: That a man found a man with his wife and killed them both. Umar wrote a letter in public: Retaliate against him; and a letter in secret: Give him the blood-money."
"17346","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: And a man informed me, from Makhul in part, he said: A man from Khuzah found a man from Aslam in his house after the night prayer (atama), wrapped in a mat. He brought him to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he lashed him one hundred and exiled him a year."
"17347","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: I heard Makhul narrate: That a man found in his house a man after the night prayer, wrapped in a mat. Umar struck him one hundred."
"17348","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah: That a man called Jundub took a youth from the youth of his people called Sabrah in his house, beat him severely, tied him, and crushed his testicles with a stone pestle. His people went to Sufyan ibn Abdullah, who was a governor over them for Umar, and he voided everything Sabrah was afflicted with. His people went and came to Umar at Dajnan. Sabrah said: O Commander of the Faithful, Jundub took me at my paternal cousin asking her for dinner, and did to me such and such. Sufyan voided that. Umar said to Sufyan: Ask about this; if it was after the night prayer, lash him one hundred lashes."
"17349","From Ibrahim, he said: Amr ibn Abi Jafar informed me, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Jundub: That he took a man in his house and crushed his testicles, and Umar voided it (no liability). He said: And Salih ibn Kaysan informed me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: That a man found a man in his house and pounded every joint of his back, and Umar ibn al-Khattab voided it."
"17350","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hayyan al-Abdi asked Ata about a man who wounds his slave and breaks him? He said: Let him clothe him a garment, or let him feed him something. Hayyan said: Thus Jabir ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Abbas. Hayyan said: Then he puts out his eye? He said: More beloved to me that he free him."
"17351","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever mutilates a slave of his, he is freed."
"17352","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: A man kindled fire in the belly (inside) of his slave; the slave stood frightened until he came to a well and threw himself into it. In the morning he came to Umar: So he freed him, and captives were brought to Umar after that, and he gave him a slave. Al-Hasan said: They used to punish and to compensate — meaning: when he freed him, Umar compensated him in his place."
"17353","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that a man branded a boy of his with fire, and Umar freed him."
"17354","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Sufyan ibn al-Aswad ibn Abd al-Aswad fell upon a slave-girl of his and seated her upon a frying pan, so her buttocks burned. Umar ibn al-Khattab freed her and pained him with beating."
"17355","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from a man among them, from Umar, that a man seated a slave-girl of his upon fire, and Umar freed her."
"17356","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather Abdullah ibn Amr, that Zinba — Abu Rawh ibn Zinba — found a slave of his with a slave-girl, cut his penis and cut his nose. The slave came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What carried you to this? He said: He did such and such. He said: Go, for you are free. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah al-Arzami narrate it, from Amr ibn Shuayb."
"17357","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard Ikrimah informed me, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Abu Masud al-Ansari while he was beating his servant. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said: Know, Abu Masud! When he heard him he threw the whip. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, Allah is more able over you than you over this one. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to mutilate his slave, blinding or cutting. And he said: Feed them full and do not starve them, clothe them and do not leave them bare, do not beat them much, for you will be asked about them; do not overburden them with work. Whoever hates his slave, let him sell him, and let him not make Allah's provision upon him a hardship."
"17358","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed us, he said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hush! The sky has creaked. He said: And I was informed that he said: And it has right to creak; there is no place of a palm in the sky — or he said: a span — except upon it is an angel prostrating. So fear Allah, and do good to what your right hands possess: feed them from what you eat, clothe them from what you wear, do not burden them what they cannot bear; and if they bring something of manners opposing something of your manners, then entrust their evil to other than you, and do not punish Allah's servants."
"17359","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah ibn Asim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the Farewell Pilgrimage: Your slaves, your slaves; feed them from what you eat, clothe them from what you wear; and if they bring a sin you do not wish to forgive, then sell Allah's servants, and do not punish them."
"17360","From al-Thawri, from Firas, from Abu Salih, from Zadham, he said: I was sitting with Ibn Umar, and he called a slave of his and freed him. He said: I have none of his reward weighing this, or equaling this — taking something in his hand — that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever beats a slave of his for a prescribed punishment he did not commit, or slaps him, his expiation is to free him."
"17361","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Muawiyah ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, from Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, he said: We, the sons of Muqarrin, were seven in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we had a servant, we had none besides her. One of us slapped her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Free her. We said: We have no servant besides her, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She serves you until you can do without her, then let her go free."
"17362","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to beat women and servants. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Umar, the same."
"17363","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: Nafi was asked: Did Ibn Umar beat his slaves? He said: Yes; and he would free such-and-such in a single hour."
"17364","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, that al-Zubayr used to beat his wives until he would break upon one of them the sticks of the clothes-rack."
"17365","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never struck a servant of his, nor a woman, nor struck anything with his hand ever, except to strive in the way of Allah; nor was he given choice between two matters ever, except the more beloved to him was the easier of them, so long as it was not a sin; and if it was a sin, he was the furthest of people from sin; nor did he take revenge for himself for anything done to him, until the sanctity of Allah was violated, and then he would take revenge for Allah."
"17366","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is not one of you ashamed to beat his wife as he beats the slave — beating her at the start of the day, then lying with her at its end; is he not ashamed? From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from Urwah, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"17367","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, from Iyas ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Dhubab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not beat Allah's bondwomen. He said: Women became emboldened, and their manners worsened toward their husbands. Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, women have become emboldened, and their manners worsened toward their husbands since you forbade beating them. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Beat them. People beat their wives that night. Many women came complaining of beating. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the morning: Seventy women circled the family of Muhammad last night, all complaining of beating. By Allah, you will not find those (beaters) the best of you."
"17368","From Ma'mar, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: I served the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ten years; no, by Allah, he never insulted me with an insult ever, nor said to me: Uff, ever; nor said to me for something I did: Why did you do it? Nor for something I did not do: Why did you not do it!"
"17369","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, he said: Thabit narrated to me, from Anas, he said: I heard him say: I served the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ten years; no, by Allah, he never said to me for something I did: Why did you do it? Nor for something I did not do: Why did you not do it! Nor blamed me; and if one of his family blamed me, he said: Leave him; what is decreed, it shall be — or: what is ordained, it shall be."
"17370","From Ma'mar, he said: Al-Zuhri was asked about beating servants, and he said: They used to beat them but not curse them."
"17371","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a donkey branded on its face, its nostrils smoking, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah curses whoever did this. Let no one brand the face, and let no one strike the face."
"17372","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you strike, beware of the face; for Allah created the face of Adam upon His image."
"17373","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Atiyyah al-Awfi, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you fights, let him avoid the face."
"17374","From Yahya al-Bajali, from Ibn Ajlan, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you strikes, let him avoid the face, and let him not say: May Allah disgrace your face, and the face of whoever resembles your face; for Allah created Adam upon His image."
"17375","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that he forbids a man saying to a man: May Allah disgrace your face."
"17376","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: No man strikes a slave of his unjustly, except retaliation is taken from him on the Day of Resurrection."
"17377","From Ma'mar, he said: I saw with al-Zuhri a slave of his, a Berber, shackled with iron."
"17378","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Abu Hurayrah used to say: The severest of people against a man on the Day of Resurrection is his slave."
"17379","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: Once a man was beating a slave of his while he was saying: I seek refuge with Allah — when he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I seek refuge with the Messenger of Allah. He threw what was in his hand and let the slave go. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed, by Allah, Allah is more deserving that whoever seeks refuge with Him be protected, than I. He said: The man said: O Messenger of Allah, then he is for the sake of Allah. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, had you not done it, your face would have met the scorch of the Fire."
"17380","From al-Thawri, he said: ... informed me, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, he said: Abu Dharr passed by a man beating a slave of his, and Abu Dharr said to him: I know what you will say to your Lord, and what He will say to you: You say: O Allah, forgive me — and He says: Did you forgive? Then you say: O Allah, have mercy on me — and He says: Did you have mercy?"
"17381","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Masud al-Ansari, he said: Once I was beating a slave of mine, when I heard a voice behind me: Know, Abu Masud; know, Abu Masud — three times. I turned, and it was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: By Allah, Allah is more able over you than you over this one. So I swore never to beat a slave of mine ever."
"17382","From Sufyan al-Thawri, he said: Al-Shabi said to me: I never struck a slave of mine ever."
"17383","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Al-Shabi said: If you hear me say to my slave, May Allah disgrace you, then he is free."
"17384","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: It used to be said: Do not combine upon servants the night and the day (i.e., work them day and night)."
"17385","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Dawud ibn Ali, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hang the whip where the people of the house see it."
"17386","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Humayd ibn Nafi, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I dislike seeing a man kindling (with anger), twisting a neck, standing at his threshing-floor (? maras) beating her."
"17387","Yahya informed us, he said: Al-Amash narrated to us, from Marur ibn Suwayd, he said: I passed by al-Rabadhah and saw Abu Dharr wearing a cloak, and his slave wearing its mate. I said: O Abu Dharr, had you joined these two it would be a suit. He said: I shall inform you about that. I insulted a man among my companions, and his mother was non-Arab, and I disparaged her. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to excuse himself from me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Dharr, indeed in you is ignorance. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, at this age of mine, of old age? He said: You are a man in whom is ignorance. They are your brothers; Allah made them youths for you under your hands. Whoever has his brother under his hand, let him feed him from his food, clothe him from his clothes, and not burden him what overcomes him; and if he does, let him help him with it."
"17388","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, from Abd al-Karim, from Mujahid, that Abu Dharr used to pray wearing a cotton cloak and a shawl, and he had a small flock, and his slave wore a cotton cloak and a shawl. It was said to him, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Feed them from what you eat, clothe them from what you wear, do not burden them what they cannot bear; and if you do, then help them; and if you dislike them, sell them and exchange them; and do not punish a creation like yourselves."
"17389","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Ajlan, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Ajlan Abu Muhammad, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah narrate, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: For the slave is his food and his clothing; and do not burden him of work except what he can bear."
"17390","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed me that he heard: That a person, if he beats his slave more than forty lashes, then he has transgressed."
"17391","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to shackle his slave with a light shackle."
"17392","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: That a woman slandered a slave-girl, saying to her: O adulteress — or a man slandered his slave-girl. Abdullah ibn Umar said: Did you see her commit adultery? He said: No. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall surely be lashed for her on the Day of Resurrection eighty."
"17393","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Yahya, he said: Rabiah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: Whoever slanders his slave-girl is lashed on the Day of Resurrection eighty lashes with a whip of iron."
"17394","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah: That a woman slandered her slave-girl, saying: O adulteress — or a man slandered his slave-girl. Abdullah ibn Umar said: Did you see her commit adultery? He said: No. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall surely be lashed for her on the Day of Resurrection eighty."
"17395","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: And the woman is killed for the man; there is no excess between them. And Amr."
"17396","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The wife is not subjected to retaliation from her husband in discipline — saying: If he beats her and wounds her; but if he transgresses against her and kills her, there is retaliation."
"17397","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab killed a man for a woman."
"17398","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: And the woman is subjected to retaliation from the man in every deliberate act reaching a life and above of wounds; but if they settle upon blood-money, it is paid in the blood-money of the woman in her blood-money; and whatever exceeds in the settlement in her blood-money, the aqilah bears nothing of it unless they wish."
"17399","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no retaliation between men and women except in life; nor between free and slaves retaliation except in life."
"17400","From al-Thawri, from Jafar ibn Burqan, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote that retaliation is between man and woman in deliberate acts even in life. Sufyan said: Retaliation is in life, and less than it, between man and woman — in the saying of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"17401","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Ali, he said: Whatever occurs between a man and a woman, in it is retaliation for wounds, or killing a soul, or other than that, when it is deliberate."
"17402","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, from Ali, that between them is six thousand."
"17403","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Retaliation applies between men and women in deliberate injury. He said: And Jabir said it, from al-Shabi."
"17404","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no retaliation between men and women except for the soul, and no retaliation between freemen and slaves except for the soul."
"17405","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: And wounds are retaliation, and the ruler has no right to beat him nor imprison him; it is only retaliation, and Allah was not forgetful; had He willed He would have commanded beating and imprisonment."
"17406","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata and Ibn Abi Mulaykah, they said: If a man kills a man, and the slain man had wounded the killer, the killer is killed, and the family of the slain pays blood-money for what he wounded the killer."
"17407","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Retaliation is waited until its owner recovers."
"17408","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah informed them, that a man stabbed a man with a horn in his leg, so he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Grant me retaliation. He said: Not until you heal. He said: Grant me retaliation, so he granted him retaliation. Then he became lame, and the retaliator came and said: My right. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: You have nothing. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Talhah, the like. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: May Allah remove you far! You hastened."
"17409","From al-Thawri, from Humayd al-Araj, from Mujahid, that a man struck a man with a horn in his thigh, so he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and asked him to grant him retaliation. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Until you heal. But he refused except that he grant him retaliation, so he granted him retaliation, then it broke open and his leg became paralyzed afterwards. So he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: I do not see anything for you; you have taken your right."
"17410","From al-Thawri, from Isa ibn al-Mughirah, from Yazid ibn Wahb, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Tarif ibn Rabiah, and he was a judge in Sham, that Safwan ibn al-Muattal struck Hassan ibn Thabit with the sword, so the Ansar came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Retaliation. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: You wait; if your companion recovers you retaliate, and if he dies we grant you. Then he recovered, so the Ansar said: You know that the desire of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is pardon. He said: So they pardoned him, and Safwan gave him a slave-girl, and she is the mother of Abd al-Rahman ibn Hassan."
"17411","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged concerning a man who stabbed another with a horn in his leg, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, grant me retaliation. So he said: Until your wound heals. But the man refused except to retaliate, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, granted him retaliation, and the one retaliated upon recovered, and the retaliator became lame. So he said: I became lame and my companion recovered. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Did I not command you not to retaliate until your wound heals, but you disobeyed me; may Allah remove you far, and your lameness is void. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded: Whoever has a wound after the man who became lame is not to retaliate until the wound of his companion heals. So wounds are according to what they reach when they heal; whatever is paralysis or lameness, there is no retaliation in it, and it is blood-money. And whoever retaliates a wound and the one retaliated upon is harmed, then the blood-money of what exceeds his blood-money for the wound of his companion is his. I heard al-Muthanna say: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me of it."
"17412","From Mamar, from a man, who heard Ikrimah, he said: A man stabbed a man with a horn, so he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Grant me retaliation. So he said: Leave it until you heal. Then he repeated it to him twice or thrice, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Leave it until you heal. Then he granted him retaliation for it. Then the retaliator became lame, so he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: My companion recovered and I became lame. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Did I not command you not to retaliate until your wound heals. So wounds are according to what they reach, and whatever is paralysis or lameness, there is no retaliation in it, and it is blood-money. And whoever retaliates a wound and the one retaliated upon is harmed, then the blood-money of what falls short of the wound of his companion is his. And he judged that wala is for the one who frees."
"17413","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man retaliates from a man before his companion heals, then the retaliator dies from what hit him. He said: I see that the excess of the blood-money of the retaliator is paid."
"17414","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man retaliates from a man, then the one retaliated upon dies. He said: I see that it is to be paid. He said: And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I think it will be paid."
"17415","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If a man retaliates from another, then the one retaliated upon dies, he pays his blood-money."
"17416","From Mamar, and Ibn Abi Najih, from Ibn Shihab, he said: The Sunnah is that it is paid."
"17417","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who paralyzed the finger of a man, he said: He retaliates from him; if his finger becomes paralyzed, and otherwise he pays him the blood-money."
"17418","From Hushaym, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, or another, I doubt, from al-Shabi, concerning a man who wounded a man and he retaliated from him, then the one retaliated upon perished. He said: His blood-money is upon the one who retaliated from him, and the blood-money of his wound is deducted from that; whatever remains of his blood-money is upon him."
"17419","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubrumah, from al-Harith al-Ukli, concerning the one retaliated upon who then dies. He said: He pays his blood-money, because the soul is a mistake."
"17420","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: Whoever dies under retaliation, there is no blood-money for him."
"17421","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Umar, he said: His killing is right, meaning: that there is no blood-money."
"17422","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: He used to narrate it from some of them, saying: He does not pay it; it is only a prescribed penalty that met its term."
"17423","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Umar, he said: He is not paid; his killing is right."
"17424","Qatadah said: And a man informed me, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: The Book of Allah killed him."
"17425","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Umar and Ali, they said: He does not pay him. Or one of them said: His killing is right, and the other said: The Book of Allah killed him."
"17426","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Umayr ibn Sad, he said: Ali said: I would never carry out a hadd upon anyone and he dies, and find anything in myself, except the drinker of wine; for if he died I would pay his blood-money, and that is because the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not prescribe it as Sunnah."
"17427","From Mamar, from Said, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Masud, he said: Upon the one retaliated upon is his blood-money, except that the blood-money of his wound is deducted from him."
"17428","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad informed me, that Ali and Umar agreed upon that: Whoever dies under retaliation, there is no right for him; the Book of Allah killed him. I said to him: Who is Muhammad? He said: I think he is Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah al-Arzami."
"17429","From Mamar, from Matar al-Warraq, and others, from al-Hasan, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab sent for a secluded woman whom he would visit, and he disapproved of that, so he sent for her, and it was said to her: Answer Umar. So she said: Woe to her, what has she to do with Umar! He said: While she was on the road, she became frightened, labor seized her, so she entered a house and cast her child. The boy cried twice, then died. So Umar consulted the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and some of them advised him that there is nothing upon you; you are only a guardian and a discipliner. He said: And Ali was silent, so he turned to him and said: What do you say? He said: If they spoke by their opinion, their opinion has erred; and if they spoke to please your desire, they were not sincere to you. I see that its blood-money is upon you, for you frightened her, and she cast her child because of you. He said: So he ordered Ali to divide its blood-money upon Quraysh, meaning: take its blood-money from Quraysh because it is a mistake. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard al-Amash narrate the counsel of Ali and its dismissal, and his ordering him to strike the blood-money upon Quraysh."
"17430","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is there retaliation for the mamumah? He said: We never heard anyone retaliate for it before Ibn al-Zubayr."
"17431","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, that Ibn al-Zubayr retaliated for the mamumah."
"17432","From Mamar, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jahshi, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, he said: A man broke the thigh of a man, so I asked in Madinah, and most of those I asked ordered me with retaliation, so I retaliated from him."
"17433","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: There is no retaliation for the munaqqilah and the jaifah."
"17434","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The jaw is broken, and the spine, and the hand, and the nose. He said: There is no retaliation for any of that."
"17435","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Everything from which, if you retaliate, comes out like what he suffered equally, then retaliate from it; and everything which cannot be made like it, then do not retaliate from it. I said: The eye? He said: Yes, and the tooth. Then I mentioned that to Amr, the saying of Ata, he said: What he said is good."
"17436","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no retaliation in the jaifah nor the mamumah."
"17437","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: There is no retaliation in the mamumah."
"17438","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: There is no retaliation in the hashimah, nor the munaqqilah, nor the jaifah, nor the mamumah, nor the paralyzed hand, nor the paralyzed leg, and in that is blood-money."
"17439","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrimah, raising it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: There is no retaliation in paralysis, nor in lameness, nor in fracture, and in it is blood-money. From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like."
"17440","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hasan and al-Shabi, that they said: There is no retaliation in a bone except the head."
"17441","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: There is no retaliation in bones. From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, the like."
"17442","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that a guard of Shurayh struck a man with the whip, so he retaliated from him. Sufyan said: And our companions say: There is no retaliation in the slap, nor in its likes, nor in the whip and the stick, and in that is a just assessment."
"17443","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: There is no retaliation in the munaqqilah, the jaifah, and the mamumah, and no retaliation in breaking a bone."
"17444","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: There is no retaliation for the slap. And Ibn Abi Layla said: There is no retaliation in it."
"17445","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, they said: There is no retaliation in the slap, nor the punch."
"17446","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Mukhariq ibn Abdillah, he said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab say: The uncle of Khalid ibn al-Walid slapped a man from us, so his uncle came to Khalid and said: O assembly of Quraysh, indeed Allah has not made your faces superior to our faces, except what Allah favored His Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, by. So Khalid said: Retaliate. Then the man said to his nephew: Slap and slap hard. Then when he raised his hand he said: Leave it for Allah."
"17447","And I heard a freedman of Sulayman say: He informs Mamar: That Sulayman ibn Habib judged concerning the slap, if it reddens, or blackens, or greens, with six dinars."
"17448","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfah as a collector, then a man disputed with him concerning his charity, so Abu Jahm struck him and wounded him in the head. So they came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Retaliation, O Messenger of Allah. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For you is such and such. But they were not pleased. He said: For you is such and such. But they were not pleased. He said: Then for you is such and such. Then they were pleased. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I am going to address the people and inform them of your pleasure. They said: Yes. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, addressed, and said: These Laythis came to me seeking retaliation, so I offered them such and such, and they were pleased. Are you pleased? They said: No. Then the Muhajirun rushed at them, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered them to restrain, so they restrained. Then he called them and increased them, and said: Are you pleased? They said: Yes."
"17449","From Mamar, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, from Urwah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Abu Jahm over the spoils of Hunayn, then it reached Abu Jahm that Malik ibn al-Barsa, or al-Harith ibn al-Barsa, stole from the spoils, so Abu Jahm struck him and wounded him a munaqqilah wound. So he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asking him for retaliation. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: He struck you for a sin you committed; there is no retaliation for you. For you is one hundred sheep. But he was not pleased. He said: Then for you is two hundred sheep. But he was not pleased. He said: Then for you is three hundred; I will not increase you. I thought he said: Then the man was pleased. He said: And my knowledge is that he mentioned it from Urwah also."
"17450","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abdillah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: A collector went out in the time of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abu Jundub ibn al-Barsa and Abu Jahm ibn Ghanim went out with him. Then Abu Jundub ibn al-Barsa boasted, Salafa ibn Qays, so Abu Jahm stood to him and struck his head with two camel jawbones. Then when they came to Madinah to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, compensated Jundub and his companions, then said: Are you pleased? They said: Yes. He said: I am going to mention your pleasure upon the pulpit, and when I mention it, say: Yes. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood upon the pulpit and mentioned their pleasure. He said: Are you pleased? They said: No. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, descended and said: Did you not claim you were pleased, why did you not ask me for more. Then he increased them and said: Are you pleased? They said: Yes. He said: I am going to stand upon the pulpit and mention your pleasure, and when I ask you, are you pleased, say: Yes. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood and mentioned their pleasure, and said: Are you pleased? They said: Yes. And he did not retaliate from him."
"17451","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from al-Mughirah ibn Sulayman: That a governor of Umar struck a man, so he retaliated from him. So Amr ibn al-As said: O Commander of the Believers, do you retaliate from your governors? He said: Yes. He said: Then we will not work for you. He said: Even if you do not work. He said: Or you satisfy him. He said: Or I satisfy him."
"17452","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Abu Husayn, from Habib ibn Suhban, he said: I heard Umar say: The backs of the Muslims are the sanctuary of Allah; it is not lawful for anyone, unless a hadd removes it."
"17453","He said: And I saw the whiteness of his armpit while standing retaliating from himself."
"17454","From Mamar, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out of his house intending prayer, then a man took hold of the bridle of his camel and said: My need, O Messenger of Allah. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Leave me, for you will attain your need. Then he did that three times, and the man refused. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, raised the whip against him and struck him, and said: Leave me, you will attain your need. Then he prayed with the people, and when he finished he said: Where is the man whom I flogged just now? He said: The people looked at one another and said: Who is this whom the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, flogged? Then the man came from the last rows and said: I seek refuge with Allah from the anger of Allah and the anger of His Messenger. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Come near and retaliate! Then he threw the whip to him. He said: Rather I pardon. He said: Or you pardon? Then he said: I have pardoned. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, no believer wrongs a believer and does not give him his right in this world, except Allah avenges him from him on the Day of Resurrection. He said: Then Abu Dharr said: O Prophet of Allah, do you remember the night I was leading your mount, and when I led it it lagged, and when I drove it it turned aside, while you were dozing upon it, so I struck your head with the whip and said: Beware, beware of the people. He said: Yes. He said: Then retaliate from me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Rather I pardon. He said: Rather retaliate from me, it is dearer to me. He said: So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, struck him a blow with the whip; I saw him writhing from it."
"17455","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, met a man dyed with yellow, and in the hand of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was a palm stalk. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Put down wars. He said: Then he poked with the stalk into the belly of the man, and said: Did I not forbid you from this? He said: Then it marked his belly, but did not cause it to bleed. So the man said: Retaliation, O Messenger of Allah. So the people said: From the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, you retaliate? So he said: The skin of no one has Allah favored over my skin. He said: Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, uncovered his belly, then said: Retaliate. Then the man kissed the belly of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: I leave it for you to intercede for me with it on the Day of Resurrection."
"17456","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: There was a man from the Ansar called Sawadah ibn Amr who perfumed himself as if he were a date stalk, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he saw him, would bite for him. He said: Then he came one day perfumed, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gestured toward him with a stick in his hand and wounded him. So he said: Retaliation, O Messenger of Allah. So he gave him the stick, and upon the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, were two shirts. He said: So he began lifting them. He said: So the people rebuked him. He said: Then he uncovered until he reached the place he had wounded, then he threw the stick and clung kissing him, and said: O Prophet of Allah, rather I leave it for you to intercede for me with it on the Day of Resurrection."
"17457","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: When Umar came to Sham, a man came to him seeking justice against one of his governors, so he wanted to retaliate from him. So Amr ibn al-As said to him: Then no one will work for you. He said: Even so, shall I not retaliate from him? And I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, grant retaliation from himself. Amr said: Then why not something else, satisfy him. He said: Or I satisfy him."
"17458","Mamar and Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: We were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in a campaign, then a man from the Muhajirun kicked a man from the Ansar. So the Ansari said: O Ansar! And the Muhajiri said: O Muhajirun! So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, heard that and said: What is with the call of ignorance? Then they informed him of what happened. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Leave it, for it is foul. He said: And the Muhajirun, when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to Madinah, were fewer than the Ansar, then the Muhajirun increased afterwards. Then Abdillah ibn Ubayy heard of that. He said: They did it? By Allah, if we return to Madinah, the mighty will expel the lowly from it. He said: So Umar said: Leave me, O Messenger of Allah, to strike the neck of this hypocrite. So he said: Leave him, lest people say Muhammad kills his companions."
"17459","Muhammad ibn Muslim informed us, from Yazid ibn Abdillah ibn Usamah, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, retaliated from himself, and that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, retaliated a man from himself, and that Umar retaliated Sad from himself."
"17460","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, he said: Hafs ibn Maysarah narrated to us, he said: He traced it to me and I forgot, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out one day binding his head with a red band, leaning, or he said: reclining upon al-Fadl ibn Abbas. Then he said: Prayer is assembled. So the people gathered and he ascended the pulpit and said: I praise to you Allah, beside Whom there is no god, and rights have drawn near from me, from among you. So whoever I insulted his honor, this is my honor, let him retaliate from it. And whoever I struck his back, this is my back, let him retaliate from it. And whoever I took his property, this is my property, let him take from it. And let none of you say: I fear hatred from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Indeed it is not of my nature, nor of my character. And the dearest of you to me is he who takes a right if he has it, or absolves me, so I meet my Lord with a pure soul. Then a man stood and said: I ask you for three dirhams. So he said: From where? He said: You borrowed them on such and such day. Then he ordered al-Fadl ibn Abbas to pay them to him."
"17461","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz in it: It reached us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Any practitioner who was not known for medicine, who practices upon anyone of the Muslims, with an iron instrument, and hits a soul or less, then upon him is the blood-money of what he hit."
"17462","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Malih ibn Usamah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab held liable a man who circumcised boys, and he cut part of the boy's organ, so he held him liable. Mamar said: And I heard other than Ayyub say: A woman circumcised women and she killed a slave-girl, so Umar held her liable."
"17463","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, that Ali said concerning the physician: If he does not call witnesses to what he treats, let him blame none but himself, meaning: he is liable."
"17464","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: Ali addressed the people and said: O assembly of physicians, veterinarians, and practitioners, whoever among you treats a human or an animal, let him take for himself an exemption; for if he treated something and did not take for himself an exemption and it perished, he is liable."
"17465","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He was asked about a man who shod an animal and it became lame? He said: If he does it regularly, there is nothing upon him; and if he only undertook it and it is not his work, he is liable."
"17466","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Concerning the physician, if he does a deed with his hand, there is no liability upon him except that he transgresses."
"17467","From al-Thawri, from Yunus and Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: There is no liability upon the healer. Yunus said: From al-Shabi: Nor upon the cupper is liability."
"17468","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yunus, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard al-Shabi say: There is no liability upon a healer, nor a veterinarian, nor a cupper. And whoever enters the house of people without their permission, and their dog wounds him, there is no liability upon them; and if he enters with their permission, they are liable. And whoever peeks into the house of people without their permission and they gouge his eye, there is nothing upon them."
"17469","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The physician lances the wound and he dies in his hand. He said: There is no blood-money upon him. And Amr ibn Dinar."
"17470","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: Whoever inflicts a punishment without excess, there is no blood-money upon him."
"17471","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The slave is not retaliated from the freeman. He said: And Ibrahim said: The slave does not retaliate from the freeman."
"17472","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: A slave wounds the freeman or gouges his eye. He said: A freeman does not retaliate from a slave. And Mujahid and Sulayman ibn Musa said that."
"17473","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Salim ibn Abdillah, he said: The slave does not retaliate from the freeman, but he pays his blood-money if he kills him or wounds him; and the blood-money of the slave is in his price, like the blood-money of the freeman in his blood-money."
"17474","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: There is no retaliation between the freeman and the slave. Mamar said: And al-Zuhri said it."
"17475","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: If a freeman slaps a slave, or a slave a freeman, reconciliation is made between them with a settlement, and no retaliation between them."
"17476","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The slave is not retaliated, nor the dhimmi, from the free Muslim. From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, the like."
"17477","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no retaliation between freemen and slaves except for the soul."
"17478","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The slave is not retaliated from the freeman, and the woman is retaliated from the man in every deliberate act reaching a soul, and less than that of wounds."
"17479","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me, that there was between some of his family and the Sahmiyyun that a boy who had not reached puberty hit the tooth of a man, but he refused except to be retaliated from him. So Uthman ibn Rabiah wrote about that to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, while he governed Madinah, so he wrote that: He is not to be retaliated from."
"17480","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that: There is no retaliation, nor retribution in wounds, nor killing, nor hadd, nor exemplary punishment upon one who has not reached puberty, until he knows what is for him in Islam and what is upon him."
"17481","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning the boy who struck a man with the sword and killed him, then the boy was sought and defended himself with his sword, so a man killed him. He said: The Sunnah has passed that the deliberate act of the boy is a mistake, and whoever kills a boy who has not reached puberty, we retaliate him for him. Mamar said: What al-Zuhri said did not please me. Mamar said: Put upon his killer a blood-money for the family of the boy, and upon the kin of the boy a blood-money for the family of the slain."
"17482","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: The deliberate act of the boy is a mistake. Sufyan said: Hudud are only carried out upon one who reached puberty; the reports came with it."
"17483","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Sunnah has passed that the deliberate act of the boy is a mistake."
"17484","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me: That six men and a woman killed a man in Sanaa, so Yala wrote about them to Umar, so Umar wrote about them: Kill them all; had the people of Sanaa all killed him, I would kill them."
"17485","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar informed me, that Hayy ibn Yala informed him, that he heard Yala inform, that he wrote to Umar concerning a man and a woman who killed a man, so he wrote to him: Kill them; had the people of Sanaa all shared in his blood, I would kill them."
"17486","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say concerning the group who kill a man all together: They are killed for him."
"17487","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Two men are killed for a man."
"17488","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that Umar ibn al-Khattab retaliated the man for three from Sanaa, and said: Had the people of Sanaa colluded against him, I would kill them. Al-Zuhri said: Then the Sunnah passed after that that only one is killed."
"17489","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Umar killed seven for one in Sanaa."
"17490","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Seven men who killed a man in Sanaa were raised to Umar. He said: So he killed them for him, and said: Had the people of Sanaa colluded against him, I would kill them for him. He said: And Mansur informed me, from Ibrahim, from Umar the like. Sufyan said: And by it we take."
"17491","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdillah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me: That a woman in Yemen had six lovers, and she said: You cannot have that from her until you kill her husband's son. So they said: Hold him for us with you. So she held him, and they killed him with her and threw him into a well. Then the flies pointed to him, so they took him out, and they confessed his killing. So Yala ibn Umayyah wrote of their affair thus to Umar ibn al-Khattab, so Umar wrote: Kill them, the woman and them; had the people of Sanaa all killed him, I would kill them for him."
"17492","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr informed me, that Hayy ibn Yala informed him, that he heard Yala inform of this report. He said: The name of the slain is Asil, and they threw him into a well in Ghamdan. Then the green flies pointed to him. Then his father's wife circled upon a donkey in Sanaa for days, saying: O Allah, do not hide who killed Asil. Umar said: Yala used to say: She had one lover, then he and his father's wife killed him. So Hayy said: I heard Yala say: Umar wrote to me: Kill them; had the people of Sanaa all shared in his blood, I would kill them. Ibn Jurayj said: And Abd al-Karim informed me, that Umar doubted about her, until Ali said to him: O Commander of the Believers, what if a group shared in stealing a camel, and this took a limb and this a limb, would you cut them? He said: Yes. He said: Then at that his opinion approved it. Ibn Jurayj said: And Abu Bakr informed me, with the like of the report of Abd al-Karim, from Ali."
"17493","From Mamar, he said: Ziyad ibn Jabal informed me, from one who witnessed that. He said: There was a woman in Sanaa who had a stepson, and her husband was absent, and her stepson was with her, and she had a lover. So she said: This boy exposes us, so see what you do with him. Then they colluded against him, and they were seven with the woman. He said: I said to him: How did they collude against him? He said: I do not know, except that one of them was given a blade. He said: So they killed him and threw him into a well in Ghamdan. He said: The boy is missed. He said: Then his father's wife went out circling upon a donkey, and she is the one who killed him with the people, saying: O Allah, do not hide the blood of Asil. He said: And Yala addressed the people and said: See whether you sense this boy or he is mentioned to you. He said: Then a man passes by the well of Ghamdan after days, and there are green flies rising once from the well and descending another. So he looked over the well and found a smell he disapproved. So he came to Yala and said: I think I have found your companion for you. He said: And he informed him the report. He said: So Yala went out until he stood upon the well with the people with him. He said: Then the man who killed him, the friend of the woman, said: Lower me with a rope. He said: So they lowered him, and he took the boy and hid him in a burrow in the well, then said: Lift me. So they lifted him, and he said: I could find nothing. Then the people said: Now the smell from it is stronger than when we came. Then another man said: Lower me. Then when they wanted to lower him, trembling seized the other. So they secured him and lowered their companion. Then when he descended into it, he took him out to them, then came out. Then the man, the lover of the woman, confessed, and the woman confessed, and they all confessed. So Yala wrote to Umar, and he wrote to him: Kill them; had the people of Sanaa colluded with him, I would kill them. He said: So he killed the seven."
"17494","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, concerning the group who kill a man. He said: His guardians kill whom they wish, and pardon whom they wish, and take the blood-money from whom they wish. From Mamar, from Qatadah, the like."
"17495","From Ibrahim, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Had a hundred killed a man, they would be killed for him."
"17496","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Two men are not killed for a man, nor are two hands cut for a hand. Sufyan said concerning people who cut a man. He said: There is no retaliation from them, and the blood-money is upon them all."
"17497","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When the group kills someone, they choose whichever of them they wish. He said: And others said it also."
"17498","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn al-Zubayr and Abd al-Malik would kill only one man of them, and I know of no one who killed them all, except what they said about Umar."
"17499","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said, concerning a man who killed three: Is he killed for them? Mamar said: Yes. And al-Hasan and Qatadah said it."
"17500","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man killed two free men. He said: He is for them both outright."
"17501","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, concerning two men who killed a man. He said: They are both for him."
"17502","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Ali, concerning a man who killed a man and another confined him. He said: The killer is killed, and the other is imprisoned in prison until he dies. From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Ali judged the like."
"17503","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man held a man for another until he killed him. He said: They mentioned that Ali used to say: The holder is held in prison until he dies, and the other is killed. I said to Ata: If he reached from him something less than the soul, is the holder held in prison until he dies? He said: There is no retaliation from the assailant, and the holder is punished, and no retaliation from him. I said: If he killed him outright? He said: I do not see that the holder is also killed. He said: I said to him: Why should he hold him, and he did not point to him, but he walked with the killer, and went and spoke, and prevented him from a beating intended for him? He said: He is not killed."
"17504","From Mamar, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, he said: I was informed a report, and I heard it firmly from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The withholder is imprisoned for death as he withheld, and the killer is killed."
"17505","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning people who gathered against a man, and some of them held him, and some of them gouged his eye. He said: The eye of the one who gouged his eye is gouged, and the others are punished a painful exemplary punishment; and if he loves blood-money, it is upon them all."
"17506","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, concerning the group who kill a man by mistake. He said: Upon each one of them is an expiation. From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan the like. From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim the like. From Mamar, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim the like."
"17507","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Shubrumah, from al-Harith al-Ukli, he said: Upon each one of them is an expiation. From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah the like."
"17508","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Nusayr and al-Salt informed me: That a man in Basrah saw a human, and thought he was a dog, so he stoned him and killed him, and he was a human. Then the people did not know who killed him. Then Adi ibn Artah came and informed him that he killed him. So he imprisoned him, and wrote about him to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz. So Umar wrote: Evil is what you did when you imprisoned, though he came of himself and informed you that he killed him. So free his way, and put his blood-money upon the kin. And al-Salt claimed that they were both from al-Asad, the killer and the slain, and that the slain was a night patrolman patrolling."
"17509","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard some of our companions mention, that al-Harith ibn Abi Rabiah was consulted concerning a man who called his wife to sit upon his organ, and she tore it. So he judged upon him the blood-money between them in two halves."
"17510","From al-Thawri, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, from al-Shabi, he said: Ibn Ashwa asked him about a man who made his wife kneel and had intercourse with her and broke her front tooth. Al-Shabi said: He pays."
"17511","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who married a slave-girl, and entered upon her secretly from her family, and frightened her and she died. He said: Upon him is her blood-money for his falling upon her before she could bear."
"17512","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Makhul, he said: If a freeman and a slave kill a freeman by mistake, his blood-money is from the account of the price of the slave, and the share of the freeman in his blood-money."
"17513","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A freeman and a slave killed a freeman deliberately. He said: The freeman is killed for him, and the slave is for his family."
"17514","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning a freeman and a slave who killed a man deliberately. He said: They are both killed for him. Sufyan said: They are both killed for him when it is deliberate; and if it is a mistake, the slave is taken whole, and upon the freeman is half the blood-money, unless they agree to the slave that they ransom him."
"17515","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning a freeman and a slave who killed a freeman. He said: The blood-money is upon the freeman, except what reaches the price of the slave. He said: And other than Mujahid said: It is between them in halves."
"17516","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If they wish they kill the freeman and enslave the slave; and if they wish they kill them both; and if they wish they pardon one and kill the other."
"17517","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a freeman killed by a freeman and a slave. He said: The freeman is killed; and if the family of the slain wishes, they kill the slave, and if they wish they employ him."
"17518","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Farwah informed me, from his father, that some people were watering camels in a pass of the passes of Makkah, then the camels hit two men, a slave and a freeman. So Abd al-Malik judged their blood-money between them by shares: the price of the slave and the freeman upon the price of the slave and the blood-money of the freeman."
"17519","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning a slave who killed a freeman by mistake. He said: If the family of the slave wishes, they surrender the slave for his offense, and if they wish they ransom him with the blood-money of the freeman. From Mamar, from Qatadah the like."
"17520","Qatadah said: And if it is deliberate, the family of the slain have more right to the slave; if they wish they kill him, and if they wish they enslave him."
"17521","From Mamar, it reached him, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: If his master wishes, he ransoms him with the price of the slave."
"17522","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: Slaves, their practice is the practice of freemen in retaliation."
"17523","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A slave killed a freeman deliberately. He said: The slave is for them. I said: The master of the slave wanted to give the blood-money and ransom his slave, but the family of the freeman refused except the slave. He said: They have more right to him; he is for them; he refused except that. I said: If they afterwards want to hand over to them his killing? He said: They kill him if they wish. Then I said: Is a slave killed for a freeman? He said: That is disliked."
"17524","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A slave gouged the eye of a freeman; do you approve that he retaliate him? He said: No."
"17525","From al-Thawri, from Tariq, from al-Shabi, he said: The offense of the slave is in his neck; if his masters wish they surrender him for his offense, and if they wish they pay on his behalf."
"17526","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said concerning a slave who killed a man. He said: If the guardians of the slain wish, they enslave the slave. He said: And Ibrahim said: They have only retaliation or pardon. And by the saying of Ibrahim Sufyan takes. And Ibn Jurayj said, from Ata, the like of the saying of al-Shabi."
"17527","I heard Abu Hanifah asked about a runaway slave who killed a man by mistake. So he said: Hammad informed me, from Ibrahim, he said: He is handed to the guardians of the slain; if they wish they kill him, and if they wish they pardon him. Then if they pardon him, he belongs to his first masters; the family of the slain have no right to enslave him."
"17528","From Ibrahim, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali, he said: If they wish they enslave him."
"17529","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Slaves killed a freeman deliberately; is their practice the practice of freemen who kill a freeman deliberately? He said: I see only that they belong to his family, because they are property, not like freemen who killed a freeman."
"17530","Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I see slaves who kill a freeman deliberately only like freemen who kill a freeman deliberately; for them is one of them."
"17531","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A freeman killed a freeman and a slave by mistake? He said: The blood-money of the freeman and the blood-money of the slave. I said: Deliberately? He said: He is killed for the freeman, and pays the slave, unless the Sunnah passed with other than that. And had he killed two freemen, he would be outright. He said: I said: How is he killed for the freeman and the family of the freeman pays the price of the slave? And I know this only from Amr ibn Shuayb. He said: I know only that he is killed for the freeman, and pays the price of the slave."
"17532","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Concerning a freeman who killed a freeman and a slave deliberately: He is killed for the freeman, and pays the price of the slave from his property."
"17533","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a slave shared between two men, one of them freed him and the other killed him. He said: He is in the position of the freeman; the freer pays to the one who killed half his blood-money, and his blood-money is upon the killer for his heirs. Mamar said: And al-Zuhri said: He is a slave until they all free him."
"17534","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a man and a boy who killed a man deliberately. He said: The killer is killed, and the blood-money is upon the family of the boy; indeed the deliberate act of the boy is a mistake. Al-Hasan said: Blood-money and no killing."
"17535","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Many of the people said: He is not killed for him, because he does not know; perhaps the boy is the one who killed him. Just as had we sent a trained dog upon game, then an untrained dog met the game with this dog, and they joined in killing it, it is not eaten."
"17536","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Concerning an adult and a minor who killed a man. He said: Neither of them is killed, because it is not known which of them finished him. And upon them is the blood-money: the share of the minor upon the kin, and the share of the other in his property. And Ibrahim said it."
"17537","From Mamar, he said: And Hisham said, from al-Hasan: When deliberate enters into mistake, it is the blood-money."
"17538","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, narrating it from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: Whoever kills his slave, we kill him; and whoever mutilates him, we mutilate him. Then they reviewed him. He said: Allah decreed the soul for the soul."
"17539","From al-Thawri, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyab about a man who killed a slave deliberately. He said: He is killed for him. Then I returned to him, and he said: Had the people of Yemen gathered upon him, I would kill them."
"17540","From Ibn Saman, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, that Zayd ibn Aslam and Ali ibn Abi Kathir sent him to Ibn al-Musayyab asking him about that. He said: He is killed for him. He said: Then I returned to them and informed them. They said: You erred; return and ask him! He said: Then I returned to him and asked him. So he said: Who are you? He said: So I informed him. So he said: He is killed for him, O son of my brother! Had they been a hundred, I would kill them for him. From Mamar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: He is killed for him."
"17541","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih and Amr ibn Dinar, or one of them, from Ibn Abbas. Abd al-Razzaq said: And Ibn Saman informed us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying: And We prescribed for them therein that a soul is for a soul. He said: Then Ibn Saman informed me, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: He prescribed that upon the Children of Israel; this verse is for us and for them."
"17542","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: He is killed for him when it is deliberate. Al-Thawri said: If he kills his slave or the slave of another, he is killed for him. And it is our saying."
"17543","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no retaliation between the freeman and the slave, but punishment, and exemplary deterrence, and payment of what he hit."
"17544","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who killed his slave deliberately. He said: He is punished a painful punishment and imprisoned."
"17545","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who killed his own slave. He said: He is not killed for him."
